156216afda7f9b892e190e3bf2864cbfa57de8b74f26f076e1ebe2c.png)" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186088035 1068 #edb/ref 17592186084914 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186088035 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281060 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186088035 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186088035 1071 "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186088035 1071 "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n$x = 7\\cos θ, y = 3\\sin θ, 0 \\le θ < 2π$,\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y$?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186088035 1074 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -7 & \\le 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 7 \\\\ -7 & \\le x\\le 7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-7\\le x\\le 7.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -3 & \\le 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 3 \\\\ -3 & \\le y\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-3\\le y\\le 3.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $-7\\le x\\le 7$ and $-3\\le y\\le 3.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186092353 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281519 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186092355 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281538 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186100825 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281437 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186100825 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281438 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186100826 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281468 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186100828 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281481 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186100828 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281482 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186100848 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281490 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186100905 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281326 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186100906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281295 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186101237 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098727 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186101237 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280947 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186101237 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186101237 1071 "In degrees, find the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\sqrt{3}.$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186101237 1071 "In degrees, find the general solution to the equation $\\tan x =-\\sqrt{3}$. Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186101237 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=-{60}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of ${180}^{∘}.$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=-{60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186105901 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281412 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186105901 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281413 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186105901 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281414 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186105901 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281415 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186105901 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281416 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186105901 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281417 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186105901 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281418 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186105901 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281419 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186105905 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281395 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186105905 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281396 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186105905 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281397 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186105905 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281398 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186105905 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281399 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186105905 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281400 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186105905 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281401 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186105905 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281404 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186105905 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281406 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186105905 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281408 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186106073 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102294 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186106073 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281258 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186106073 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186106073 1071 "![](images/e2/e2ab6906100a96a123b4b1520613f1e38fe2c7e77af390a8efe0596a2861727d.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T$?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186106073 1071 "![](images/e2/e2ab6906100a96a123b4b1520613f1e38fe2c7e77af390a8efe0596a2861727d.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186106073 1074 "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-3)-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. Therefore, the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a line segment." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186106075 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102306 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186106075 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280959 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186106075 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186106075 1071 "![](images/e6/e6d0ce147aec90c3840a893e36596fbadf981ff96559f65d055f4486406cca7f.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T$?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186106075 1071 "![](images/e6/e6d0ce147aec90c3840a893e36596fbadf981ff96559f65d055f4486406cca7f.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T?$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186106075 1074 "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a nonsingular matrix. Therefore, the image of our object under the action of  $T$ is a quadrilateral." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186115360 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281534 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186115360 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281535 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186115360 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281536 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186115360 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281537 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186115361 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281523 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186115361 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281524 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186115361 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281525 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186115361 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281526 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186115361 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281527 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186115361 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281528 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186115361 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281529 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186115361 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281530 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186115362 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281516 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186115362 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281517 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186115362 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281518 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186115362 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281520 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186115362 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281521 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186115362 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281522 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186115522 1068 #edb/ref 17592186112111 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186115522 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281031 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186115522 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186115522 1071 "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t) = ti + \\csc (2t)j + (1 + t^{2})k$ is\n$t ∈ (- ∞, ∞), t \\ne A A A A A$.\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186115522 1071 "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=t\\,i+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2t)\\,j+(1+{t}^{2})\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne .\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186115522 1074 "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2t)$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ except for the values of $t$ for which\n\n$$\n2t=\\pi n\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{\\pi n}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $1+{t}^{2}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi n}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186119767 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281471 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [1