5 17592186045585 true] [17592186124618 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281551 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186124634 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281464 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186124651 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281540 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186124651 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281541 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186124651 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281542 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186124651 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281545 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186124651 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281546 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186124651 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281547 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186124818 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121475 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186124818 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281125 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186124818 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186124818 1071 "![](images/e1/e1f3255f450e4761e20b64c0782a241a7d73637c9e276fa9c1e07227f26cf4e2.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z = f(x, y)$ is shown above.\nWhat is the global minimum of the function?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186124818 1071 "![](images/e1/e1f3255f450e4761e20b64c0782a241a7d73637c9e276fa9c1e07227f26cf4e2.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above.\nWhat is the global minimum of the function?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186124818 1074 "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=-3.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-3.$ The function reaches this value at both $(x,y)=(1,-1)$ and $(x,y)=(1,2).$\n\n![](images/1d/1d257445551687ded71409efdce1b8695a9813a73ecb0fde5b6f4efccc6c15be.png)" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186124823 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121505 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186124823 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281147 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186124823 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186124823 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x, y) = x^{2} + (y + 2)^{2}$ on the ellipse $x^{2} + 2y^{2} - 2 = 0$." 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186124823 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\,$ on the ellipse ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2=0.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186124823 1074 "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y+4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,4y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y+4=4\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(2\\lambda -1)=2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-2 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-1),(0,1).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2(1)-1)=2\\,⇒\\,y=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,-1),\\,(0,1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,-1)$ | $(0,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $9$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the point $(0,1)$ and its maximum value is $9.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186129211 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281291 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186129211 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281292 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186129211 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281293 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186129211 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281294 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186129212 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281305 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186129212 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281306 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186129212 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281307 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186129212 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281320 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186129212 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281322 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186129212 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281612 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186129213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281298 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186129213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281299 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186129213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281300 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186129213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281301 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186129213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281302 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186129213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281303 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186129217 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281637 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186129217 1038 #edb/ref 1