186281427 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186133932 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281428 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186133932 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281430 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186133933 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281459 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186133933 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281460 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186133933 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281463 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186133934 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281442 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186133934 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281444 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186133934 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281560 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186133934 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281561 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186133934 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281562 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186133935 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281509 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186133935 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281510 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186133936 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281505 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186133936 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281506 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186133936 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281507 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186133936 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281508 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186133937 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281511 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186133937 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281512 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186133937 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281513 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186133937 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281514 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186134135 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130936 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186134135 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281264 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186134135 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186134135 1071 "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x, y, z) = yi - xj$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t) = \\cos (πt)i + \\sin (πt)j + (1 - t^{2})k$ for $t ∈ [- 1, 1)$." 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186134135 1071 "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+(1-{t}^{2})\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186134135 1074 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=1-{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i-x\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j-2t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j-2t\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+0\\cdot (-2t) \\\\  & =-\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)-\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-\\pi )\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\pi [t]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\pi (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186134142 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130978 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186134142 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280909 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186134142 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186134142 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $y =-1$, $y = 1$, $z = 0$, $z = 1$, and the surfaces $x = y - 2z$ and $x = 4 - z^{2} - y^{2}$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F = xy^{2}i + xzj + yk$, the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS =$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186134142 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $y=-1,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1,$ and the surfaces $x=y-2z$ and $x=4-{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=x{y}^{2}\\,i+xz\\,j+y\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186134142 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+0+0 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type II solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,y-2z\\le x\\le 4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186134143 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130984 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186134143 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281167 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186134143 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186134143 1071 "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by $x^{2} + (y - 2)^{2} + (z + 1)^{2} = 25$ and the vector field $F = xi + xzj + 3zk$. Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S$." 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186134143 1071 "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=25$ and the vector field $F=x\\,i+xz\\,j+3z\\,k.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186134143 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3z) \\\\  & =1+0+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $5$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {5}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\,000\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186134146 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131002 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186134146 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281277 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186134146 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186134146 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x = 0$, $x = 2$, $y = 0$, $y = 1$, $z = 0$, $z = \\frac{π}{2}$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F = x^{2}\\cos zi + \\sin zj + (x + y)k$, find the flux of $F$ across $S$." 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186134146 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,j+(x+y)\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186134146 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+y) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+0+0 \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=2}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}⁡z]_{z=0}^{z=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =({2}^{2}-{0}^{2})\\cdot 1\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186162160 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281563 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186162160 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281564 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186162161 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281539 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186162161 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281559 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186162161 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281689 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186162463 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159950 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186162463 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281229 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186162463 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186162463 1071 "| $f$ | $Y = 0$ | $Y = 1$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X = 0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X = 1$ | $0.1$ | $0.6$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x, y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n| $x$$0$$1$$f_{X}(x)$$0.3$$0.7$ |  | $y$$0$$1$$f_{Y}(y)$$0.2$$0.8$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $P(X = 1, Y = 1) = P(X = 1) \\cdot P(Y = 1)$\n2. $P(X = x, Y = y) = P(X = x) \\cdot P(Y = y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n3. $X$ and $Y$ are independent\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186162463 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.6$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n| | $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ | |  | | $y$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ | |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=1,Y=1)=P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186162463 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=1,Y=1)=f(1,1)=0.6.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.7\\cdot 0.8=0.56.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(X=1,Y=1)\\ne P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1),\n$$\n\nand $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186166888 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281393 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186166888 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281394 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186166888 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281498 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186166888 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281500 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186166888 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281502 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186166889 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281618 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186166889 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281620 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186166889 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281640 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186166889 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281641 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186166890 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281651 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186166890 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281652 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186166890 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281655 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186166890 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281658 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186166891 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281594 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186166891 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281659 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186166891 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281660 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186166891 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281668 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186166891 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281669 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186167138 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163876 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186167138 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281106 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186167138 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186167138 1071 "Consider the function $f(x, y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$f(x, y) = x^{2} + 2y + 1, D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 2, 0 \\le y \\le 3}$.\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a, b) ∈ D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z = f(x, y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a, b)$. Find $b$ given that $a = 1$." 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186167138 1071 "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}+2y+1,\\,D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 3}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $b$ given that $a=1.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186167138 1074 "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+2y+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}+2y+1)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}+y]_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(3{x}^{2}+12)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}+12x]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =({2}^{3}+12\\cdot 2)-0 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=2\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $a=1.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}32 & =f(1,b)\\cdot 6 \\\\ 32 & =({1}^{2}+2b+1)\\cdot 6 \\\\ 32 & =12b+12 \\\\ 20 & =12b \\\\ b & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(1,\\frac{5}{3})\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $b=\\frac{5}{3}.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186167140 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163888 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186167140 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281145 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186167140 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186167140 1071 "Find the average value of the function $f(x, y) = x^{2} - 4y^{3}$ on the region $D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 3}$." 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186167140 1071 "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-4{y}^{3}$ on the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3}.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186167140 1074 "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=1\\cdot 3=3.\n$$\n\nApplying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}-4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{x}^{2}y-{y}^{4}]_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{x}^{2}-81)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[{x}^{3}-81x]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({1}^{3}-81\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{80}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186171920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281431 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186171920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281432 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186171920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281439 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186171920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281440 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186171920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281441 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186172387 1068 #edb/ref 17592186169155 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186172387 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280969 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186172387 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186172387 1074 "Geometrically, the $3$rd roots of unity have the following properties:\n\n- all of them lie on the unit circle centered in the origin,\n\n- one of the roots is $1,$ corresponding to the point $(1,0)$ on the complex plane,\n\n- the roots form an equilateral triangle (regular $3$-sided polygon).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/c7/c70d25ac3a5ddb2d6765ef8a22f5e9774c9be06dd909d7079c3a361157881bb5.png)" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186176072 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281339 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186176072 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281349 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186176072 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281350 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186176072 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281351 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186176072 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281352 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186176072 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281353 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186176072 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281355 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186176073 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281361 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186176073 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281363 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186176073 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281365 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186176073 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281375 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186176073 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281376 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186176073 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281379 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186179779 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281402 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186179779 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281403 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186179779 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281405 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186179779 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281407 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186179779 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281409 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186179779 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281410 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186179781 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281411 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186179781 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281420 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186179781 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281421 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186179781 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281423 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186179781 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281424 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186179781 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281425 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186179781 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281426 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186180092 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178191 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186180092 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280992 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186180092 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186180092 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $T$, defined as\n\n$x = v - u, y = u + v - 1$.\n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186180092 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=v-u,\\,y=u+v-1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186180092 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}v-u \\\\ u+v-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-u+v \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 1=-2.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|-2|=2.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186180095 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178209 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186180095 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280894 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186180095 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186180095 1071 "![](images/3f/3f8c1c9854ec54dd3caf4487328d3f3c35f2d69680a8fd1c13d28125dac77d03.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat would be the image of the square shown above under the action of $T$?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186180095 1071 "![](images/3f/3f8c1c9854ec54dd3caf4487328d3f3c35f2d69680a8fd1c13d28125dac77d03.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the square shown above under the action of $T?$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186180095 1074 "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-3)-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible and the image of the square under the action of $T$ is a line segment." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186180108 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178287 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186180108 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281286 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186180108 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186180108 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186180108 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $3kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $(3t^{2} + 1)m/s^{2}$, where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x$, and $t \\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t = 1s$ and $t = 2s$." 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186180108 1074 "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity ${v}_{0},$ and final velocity ${v}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int (3{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & ={t}^{3}+t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0={0}^{3}+0+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}+t.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t=1\\,s$ and final velocity at time $t=2\\,s$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(1)={1}^{3}+1=2\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(2)={2}^{3}+2=10\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {10}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =150-6 \\\\  & =144\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186180109 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178293 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186180109 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280939 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186180109 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186180109 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's velocity is $2t\\,m/s,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186180109 1071 "A particle $P$ of mass $2kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's velocity is $2tm/s$, where $t \\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t = 1s$ and $t = 2s$." 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186180109 1074 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe particle's velocity is given by $v(t)=2t,$ where $t$ is time in seconds. So, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=2(1)=2\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)=2(2)=4\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (4)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2)^{2} \\\\  & =16-4 \\\\  & =12\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186192792 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281290 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186192793 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281308 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186192793 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281309 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186192793 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281312 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186192793 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281316 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186192793 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281317 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186192809 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281451 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186192809 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281452 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186192810 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281453 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186192810 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281454 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186192810 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281455 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186192810 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281456 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186192810 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281457 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186192810 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281458 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186192810 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281461 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186192810 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281462 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198370 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281625 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198370 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281626 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198370 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281670 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198370 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281671 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198370 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281672 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198371 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281673 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198371 1038 #edb/ref 1