441 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281609 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198441 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281613 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198441 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281614 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198441 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281615 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198441 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281616 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198624 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194345 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198624 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280911 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198624 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198624 1071 "![](images/fd/fd18335a2034613658c65186c4fc6e3e74067e730183d3e66fbacf8ab3601928.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F = ⟨0, z,-y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = ∬_(D)A A A A A A AdA$.\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198624 1071 "![](images/fd/fd18335a2034613658c65186c4fc6e3e74067e730183d3e66fbacf8ab3601928.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,z,\\,-y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198624 1074 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper paraboloid ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,R=-y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-2x)-(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-2y)+(-y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,z,-y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198627 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194363 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198627 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281154 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198627 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198627 1071 "Consider the vector field $F = ⟨1, 0,-x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloid $z = x^{2} + y^{2}$ and the plane $z = 1 + x$. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = ∬_(D)A A A A A A AdA$.\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198627 1071 "Consider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,0,\\,-x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=1+x.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198627 1074 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=1+x$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1+x)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1+x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=0,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 0+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=0,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 2x-0\\cdot 2y+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-2x-x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1-x)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x-1\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198633 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194399 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198633 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281221 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198633 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198633 1071 "![](images/fa/fa7f40460a8834c73c61575aef821157e1aff7dace359752fd98593271812c10.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x, y, z) = ⟨0, y(x^{2} + z^{2} + 4), 0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$." 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198633 1071 "![](images/fa/fa7f40460a8834c73c61575aef821157e1aff7dace359752fd98593271812c10.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,y({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4),\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198633 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $y$-component of $F=⟨0,y({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4),\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $y$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the left and right sides. \n\n![](images/1f/1f00f0e9fa8148ca3cf161322c926ea9fc16a4467991063cbdcb4f293254674d.png)\n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the right surface. On this surface, we have $y=2$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,y({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4),\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4),\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}{S}_{1} & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}2({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,z)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{D}({r}^{2}+4)\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}r({r}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}({r}^{3}+4r)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}({r}^{3}+4r)\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}+2{r}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\pi (\\frac{1}{4}+2) \\\\  & =9\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the left surface. On this surface, we have $y=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,0,z)=⟨0,\\,0({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4),\\,0⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =9\\pi +0 \\\\  & =9\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198635 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194411 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198635 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281040 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198635 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198635 1071 "The lamina $D = {(x, y): 0 \\le y \\le x, 0 \\le x \\le 1}$ has the mass density function $λ(x, y) = 3xy$. Find the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis." 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198635 1071 "The lamina $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le x,\\,0\\le x\\le 1}$ has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=3xy.$ Find the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198635 1074 "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is represented as a type I plane region and $\\lambda (x,y)=3xy.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot 3xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}3x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot {[{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198641 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194447 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198641 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281270 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198641 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198641 1071 "Consider the transformation $f: R^{2} → R^{2}$, defined as\n\n$f(x) = \\begin{bmatrix}2x_{1}x_{2} \\\\ e^{x_{1}x_{2}}\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nIf the linear approximation of $f$ at $a = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ \\ln 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$f(x) \\approx \\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}2\\ln 2 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & 2\\end{bmatrix}(x - \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ \\ln 2\\end{bmatrix})$,\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198641 1071 "Consider the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ {e}^{{x}_{1}{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf the linear approximation of $f$ at $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈[\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & 2\\end{aligned}](x-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}]),\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198641 1074 "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(2{x}_{1}{x}_{2})=2{x}_{2},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(2{x}_{1}{x}_{2})=2{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({e}^{{x}_{1}{x}_{2}})={x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}{x}_{2}},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({e}^{{x}_{1}{x}_{2}})={x}_{1}{e}^{{x}_{1}{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} & 2{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}{x}_{2}} & {x}_{1}{e}^{{x}_{1}{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{f}^{′}(a)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 2 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe linear approximation of $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nf(x)≈[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 2 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 2\\end{aligned}](x-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}]).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198644 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194465 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198644 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281114 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198644 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198644 1071 "A thin rod with mass density function $\\lambda (x)=4{x}^{2},$ measured in $\\text{kg/m},$ is placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $1\\le x\\le 2,$ where $x$ is measured in meters. Find the moment of the rod about the origin." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198644 1071 "A thin rod with mass density function $λ(x) = 4x^{2}$, measured in $kg/m$, is placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $1 \\le x \\le 2$, where $x$ is measured in meters. Find the moment of the rod about the origin." 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198644 1074 "The moment about the origin $O$ of a rod with mass density function $\\lambda (x)$ for $a\\le x\\le b$ is given by \n\n$$\n{M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=4{x}^{2},\\,a=1,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the moment of the rod about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\cdot 4{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{4}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{4}-{1}^{4} \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required moment is $15\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198646 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194477 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198646 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281098 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198646 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198646 1071 "Consider a thin rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $0 \\le x \\le 2$ whose mass density function is $λ(x) = 3x$. Given that the rod's mass is $6$ units, what is the $x$-coordinate of its center of mass?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198646 1071 "Consider a thin rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $0\\le x\\le 2$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)=3x.$ Given that the rod's mass is $6$ units, what is the $x$-coordinate of its center of mass?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198646 1074 "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=3x,\\,a=0,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot 3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =8-0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{8}{6}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198649 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194495 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198649 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281005 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198649 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198649 1071 "For the function $f: R^{2} → R$, defined as\n\n$f(x, y) = e^{x + 2y}$,\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$f(x) \\approx ∗ + \\begin{bmatrix}1 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} + \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & ∗ \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}∗ \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFind the sum of the six missing expressions." 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198649 1071 "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={e}^{x+2y},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈∗+[\\begin{aligned}1 & ∗\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & ∗ \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the six missing expressions." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198649 1074 "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+2y} & 2{e}^{x+2y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{′}(0,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+2y} & 2{e}^{x+2y} \\\\ 2{e}^{x+2y} & 4{e}^{x+2y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(0,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n1+2+x+x+2+x=3x+5.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198651 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194507 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198651 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281152 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198651 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198651 1071 "For the function of two variables\n$f(x, y) = e^{x - 2y}$,\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0, 0)$ is given by\n$f(x, y) = 1 + x + ay + \\frac{1}{2}x^{2} + bxy + cy^{2}$.\nFind the value of $a + b + c$." 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198651 1071 "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)={e}^{x-2y},$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=1+x+a\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+bxy+c{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198651 1074 "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={e}^{x-2y}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-2{e}^{x-2y} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & ={e}^{x-2y}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-2{e}^{x-2y}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-2 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =4{e}^{x-2y}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+1\\cdot x+(-2)\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-2)\\cdot xy+4\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+x+(-2)y+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+(-2)xy+2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=(-2)+(-2)+2=-2.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198654 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194525 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198654 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281231 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198654 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198654 1071 "![](images/ed/ed4f595e7613254fd0cb8f27cf0b0f7443cfbe382ab525b0ca8d527c07f528fd.png)\n\nA plate with mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the curves $y = x^{2}$ and $y = \\sqrt{x}$, as shown above. Given that the region has an area of $\\frac{1}{3}$, find the moment of inertia of the plate about the line $x = 1$." 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198654 1071 "![](images/ed/ed4f595e7613254fd0cb8f27cf0b0f7443cfbe382ab525b0ca8d527c07f528fd.png)\n\nA plate with mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=\\sqrt{x}$, as shown above. Given that the region has an area of $\\frac{1}{3},$ find the moment of inertia of the plate about the line $x=1.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198654 1074 "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/60/60ec523346df73503dd61893994de6ec31069271b1157f153bbe988c9e694798.png)\n\nThe distance between a point on $D$ and the line $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|x-1|.\n$$\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=3M.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{x}}|x-1{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{x}}(x-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-1)^{2}[y]_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-2x+1)(\\sqrt{x}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{5/2}-2{x}^{3/2}+{x}^{1/2}-{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{[\\frac{2}{7}{x}^{7/2}-\\frac{4}{5}{x}^{5/2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3M(\\frac{2}{7}-\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5M}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198656 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194537 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198656 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280902 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198656 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198656 1071 "![](images/b4/b48e086ae0e94589d557eceb25b50af9deffdec442cfb7a4e559577bce899c40.png)\n\nA triangular plate with mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the $x$-axis and the lines $x = 1$ and $y = 2x$.\nThe mass density at each point is proportional to its distance from the $x$-axis. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the line $x =-2$." 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198656 1071 "![](images/b4/b48e086ae0e94589d557eceb25b50af9deffdec442cfb7a4e559577bce899c40.png)\n\nA triangular plate with mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the $x$-axis and the lines $x=1$ and $y=2x.$\nThe mass density at each point is proportional to its distance from the $x$-axis. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the line $x=-2.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198656 1074 "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2x},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f040a5500a1415b8617300f34074bb16d895889354ed7e722dbd668a233daebb.png)\n\nAlso, the distance between a point and the line $x=-2$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|x-(-2)|=|x+2|.\n$$\n\nNext, the mass density can be written as $\\lambda (x,y)=k|y|=ky,$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find the value of $k$ by integrating the mass density function over the plate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}ky\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2x}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k\\cdot {[\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=ky=\\frac{3M}{2}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $x=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}\\frac{3M}{2}y\\,|x+2{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}\\frac{3M}{2}y(x+2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+2)^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2x}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+2)^{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{2}(x+2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{4}+8{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{[\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5}+2{x}^{4}+\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}[\\frac{2}{5}+2+\\frac{8}{3}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}[\\frac{76}{15}] \\\\  & =\\frac{38M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198657 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194543 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198657 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280900 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198657 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198657 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 2, 1 \\le y \\le 3}$. Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin." 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198657 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,1\\le y\\le 3}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198657 1074 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=2\\cdot 2=4.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{8}{3}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{4}{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{[\\frac{4}{3}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}(4+9-(\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}(13-\\frac{5}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}\\cdot \\frac{34}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{17M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{R}_{0}^{2}=\\frac{17}{3}\\,⇒\\,{R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{3}}.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198660 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194561 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198660 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280926 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198660 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198660 1071 "The lamina $D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le \\sin y, 0 \\le y \\le \\frac{π}{2}}$ has the mass density function $λ(x, y) = 6\\cos y$. Find the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis." 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198660 1071 "The lamina $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}$ has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Find the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198660 1074 "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is represented as a type II plane region and $\\lambda (x,y)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡y[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}2x\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\cdot {[{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3{sin}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[{sin}^{3}⁡y]}_{y=0}^{y=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198661 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194567 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198661 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281039 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198661 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198661 1071 "For the function of two variables\n$f(x, y) = \\cos (x - 2y)$,\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0, 0)$ is given by\n$f(x, y) = 1 + ax^{2} + bxy + cy^{2}$.\nFind the value of $a + b + c$." 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198661 1071 "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-2y),$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=1+a{x}^{2}+bxy+c{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198661 1074 "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-2y), & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-2y) \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-2y), & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-2y), & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-2y), & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+0\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(-1\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(2)\\cdot xy+(-4)\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+(-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{2}+2xy+(-2){y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=(-\\frac{1}{2})+2+(-2)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198669 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194615 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198669 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281016 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198669 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198669 1071 "![](images/5b/5b8ddb36e814517af21d790374e1fb192141bd6ef0fd99e83a147c6ed5a62aea.png)\n\nA plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the circles $x^{2} + y^{2} = 1$, $x^{2} + y^{2} = 4$, and the coordinate axes, as shown above. Its mass density at any point is given by $λ(x, y) = \\frac{3}{7}My$. Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198669 1071 "![](images/5b/5b8ddb36e814517af21d790374e1fb192141bd6ef0fd99e83a147c6ed5a62aea.png)\n\nA plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the circles ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ and the coordinate axes, as shown above. Its mass density at any point is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{3}{7}My.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198669 1074 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (r,\\theta )$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{3}{7}Mr\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M({\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot {[-\\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M\\cdot (\\frac{32}{5}-\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot (-0+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}M\\cdot \\frac{31}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{31M}{35}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{y} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{31M}{35}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{31}{35}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198670 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194621 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198670 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280903 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198670 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198670 1071 "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the lines $y = x$, $x = 2$, and the $x$-axis. Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $λ(x, y) = \\frac{3}{4}My$. Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198670 1071 "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the lines $y=x,$ $x=2,$ and the $x$-axis. Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{3}{4}My.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198670 1074 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le x}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}My\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{[\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}\\cdot \\frac{16}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{12M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{y} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{12M}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{12}{5}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198671 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194627 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198671 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281262 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198671 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198671 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 1, 4 \\le y \\le 6}$. Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139533405 17592186045585 false] [17592186198671 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,4\\le y\\le 6}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186198671 1074 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=1\\cdot 2=2.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{6}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{6}\\frac{1}{3}(1-0)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{4}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}[y]_{4}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}(6-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{R}_{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⇒\\,{R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{3}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186278590 1081 "\\frac{4}{3}" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186278590 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186278590 1084 #uuid "0007cf9c-0395-5509-b616-4be695ec13cb" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186278591 1081 "\\frac{7}{6}" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186278591 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186278591 1084 #uuid "002526ef-633f-58c1-b274-926fab0583fc" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186278592 1081 "18\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186278592 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186278592 1084 #uuid "00586df5-9953-5e7a-a58f-2329aa16a468" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186278593 1081 "\\frac{4}{5}" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186278593 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186278593 1084 #uuid "0072a6bc-bcf0-53b9-9a48-720f5054cc41" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186278594 1081 "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186278594 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186278594 1084 #uuid "0073c988-ab13-5d35-82dc-f86a2f72a0de" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186278595 1081 "III only" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186278595 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186278595 1084 #uuid "007e3d6a-4d9f-5068-9677-962c469778a6" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186278596 1081 "\\frac{2}{5}" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186278596 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186278596 1084 #uuid "00b03285-dd28-53bf-99ba-9f30ee298c69" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [1759218627