d{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ v-u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v-u=0$ defines the plane $v=u..$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281345 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281176 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281345 1069 "q-161812" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281345 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281345 1071 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ {v}^{3}+{w}^{3} \\\\ v+w\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281345 1072 #uuid "f26bca76-37a5-5f68-8e4a-4d9215b02535" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281345 1074 "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u,\\,y(u,v,w)={v}^{3}+{w}^{3},\\,z(u,v,w)=v+w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3{v}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =3{w}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3{v}^{2} & 3{w}^{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}3{v}^{2} & 3{w}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(3{v}^{2}-3{w}^{2}) \\\\  & =3({v}^{2}-{w}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 3({v}^{2}-{w}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {v}^{2}-{w}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${v}^{2}-{w}^{2}=0$ defines a pair of intersecting planes $v=w$ and $v=-w.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281346 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280983 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281346 1069 "q-161815" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281346 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281346 1071 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{u} \\\\ vw \\\\ u+w\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281346 1072 #uuid "e2d2dfd0-abce-5610-a53e-633420f43575" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281346 1074 "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=\\sqrt{u},\\,y(u,v,w)=vw,\\,z(u,v,w)=u+w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =v \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & w & v \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}|\\begin{aligned}w & v \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ w & v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}(w-0)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{w}{2\\sqrt{u}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{w}{2\\sqrt{u}} & =0 \\\\ w & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $w=0$ defines the plane $w=0,$ i.e., $uv$-plane." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281347 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281146 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281347 1069 "q-161817" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281347 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281347 1071 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v{e}^{w} \\\\ 2u+v+3w\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281347 1072 #uuid "5ae1d4bd-a84e-5282-96ec-f8259badfc91" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281347 1074 "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u,\\,y(u,v,w)=v{e}^{w},\\,z(u,v,w)=2u+v+3w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & ={e}^{w} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =v{e}^{w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{w} & v{e}^{w} \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{w} & v{e}^{w} \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(3{e}^{w}-v{e}^{w}) \\\\  & ={e}^{w}(3-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{w}(3-v) & =0 \\\\ 3-v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $3-v=0$ defines the plane $v=3.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281348 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280856 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281348 1069 "q-161818" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281348 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281348 1071 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u+3w \\\\ v+2w \\\\ {v}^{2}-u\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281348 1072 #uuid "5828c68b-3870-5e91-8f73-8351ac68b7ad" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281348 1074 "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+3w,\\,y(u,v,w)=v+2w,\\,z(u,v,w)={v}^{2}-u.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2v & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2v & 0\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(0-4v)+3(0+1) \\\\  & =3-4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 3-4v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $3-4v=0$ defines the plane $v=\\frac{3}{4}.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281349 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281172 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281349 1069 "q-161821" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281349 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281349 1071 "![](images/bd/bd321d5afaa151a8da5caa6f4ca561f93566e35ff87235c06854b56cf52c0c69.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the hemisphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ for $y\\ge 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281349 1072 #uuid "a536dd94-d69c-5fc7-af99-867a1849acf6" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281349 1074 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis,