3405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281352 1072 #uuid "2f32b25c-5dca-5f67-b0bc-f63716a5f961" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281352 1074 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/6c/6cf95f58e436577be5ad696325ae2d1e7bfd5882b4899e88806c4ae653a5c518.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:  \n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,6]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi /2]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2}.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281353 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280904 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281353 1069 "q-161826" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281353 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281353 1071 "![](images/a6/a6cb0ee7062154a2489c7fa565fe6b96cd01e5f6e7a478cce39fafda0778c7a7.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the hemisphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2$ for $z\\le 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281353 1072 #uuid "416f9e78-33a8-5304-89ab-bf8598a22e79" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281353 1074 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/d7/d7011de88ab3a4acb5125e5dd67e119f7cf8e1bf823c089b5f37632a5c54b971.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:  \n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,\\sqrt{2}]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [\\pi /2,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{2},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\pi /2\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281151 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281354 1069 "q-161831" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281354 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281354 1071 "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(-1,1)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}v{e}^{u} \\\\ u{e}^{v}\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281354 1072 #uuid "7c1651a7-19c3-5fc6-882f-8612e069dc1b" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281354 1074 "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v{e}^{u} & {e}^{u} \\\\ {e}^{v} & u{e}^{v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v{e}^{u}\\cdot u{e}^{v}-{e}^{v}\\cdot {e}^{u} \\\\  & =uv{e}^{u+v}-{e}^{u+v} \\\\  & =(uv-1){e}^{u+v} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-1,1)} & =(uv-1){e}^{u+v}\\,{|}_{u=-1\\,v=1} \\\\  & =(-1\\cdot 1-1){e}^{-1+1} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-2|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =2\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281073 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281355 1069 "q-161833" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281355 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281355 1071 "![](images/b2/b221cb7e202e72f500416481175dd55b87c6a129dbdc121e20bcfdb7f8d0d443.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the quarter-sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ for $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\le 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281355 1072 #uuid "2cf460d5-5691-56a1-870f-c5b2d51af522" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281355 1074 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/cc/cca15d8d91ebfe643777122cf41a49f2bfe3bac901b30e160cad65eb6b4b102f.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary withing the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,2]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281274 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281356 1069 "q-161834" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281356 1071 "![](images/75/7523d27fa1aad56d67cb40a47715cc42d1f371c209ab4a9e5aef15413bc69490.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}{x}^{2}\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the right one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281356 1072 #uuid "3ca34213-6867-5cbf-91a5-fe2d6e0d9276" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281356 1074 "![](images/b5/b5be03a534691cfbd06178623d5a9dc802ff8814159635b4e4bf2b2693da167c.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type III region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3-x-z},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/36/365451aa395e91cee59199b048ed59ce1fba2ee8ee569fc460ef8fe9bf3bcdfd.png)\n\nHence, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3-x-z}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x-z}{x}^{2}\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x-z}{x}^{2}\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-x-z}{x}^{2}\\,dy]dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}[y]_{y=0}^{y=3-x-z}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(3-x-z)\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}z)\\,dz\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}z)\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}z)\\,dz]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[3{x}^{2}z-{x}^{3}z-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{z}^{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})\\,dx & ={[{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281357 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281180 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281357 1069 "q-161836" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281357 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281357 1071 "![](images/b6/b6d469b24061f0f7284691b2e2f26a2c28d9557ab72a1c8ba3cad93eb65dde91.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}2{z}^{2}\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the right one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281357 1072 #uuid "a7cc8b30-5e45-52f4-b1ab-d94f0af57f15" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281357 1074 "![](images/91/9190139502ef900218e3c3b186295627af2a1370b9f7d789366dac6474251b54.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type III region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le y=3-\\frac{x}{3}-z},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/aa/aa02840c9fa15e5e5048608fff81ad9f0a5623821a57366bf12ec6d60332bc9e.png)\n\nHence, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3-\\frac{x}{3}-z}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x/3-z}2{z}^{2}\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3-x/3-z}2{z}^{2}\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-x/3-z}2{z}^{2}\\,dy]dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{z}^{2}[y]_{y=0}^{y=3-x/3-z}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{z}^{2}(3-\\frac{x}{3}-z)\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{z}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}x{z}^{2}-2{z}^{3})\\,dz\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{z}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}x{z}^{2}-2{z}^{3})\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{z}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}x{z}^{2}-2{z}^{3})\\,dz]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[2{z}^{3}-\\frac{2}{9}x{z}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{4}]}_{z=0}^{z=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(8-\\frac{16}{9}x)\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(8-\\frac{16}{9}x)\\,dx & ={[8x-\\frac{16}{18}{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=3} \\\\  & =(24-8)-0 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281358 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280876 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281358 1069 "q-161838" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281358 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281358 1071 "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u+v \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281358 1072 #uuid "613ff480-77bb-508e-adea-361b7545c613" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281358 1074 "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u+v \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (2v)-(2u)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2v-2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2v-2u & <0 \\\\ 2v & <2u \\\\ v & <u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=u\n$$\n\ndefines a straight line, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,-1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/83/8382e54c1f91ff0e07910c1250c046413814748d9e7ef8968984b7223b43afcc.png)" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281359 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281230 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281359 1069 "q-161842" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281359 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281359 1071 "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} \\\\ u-\\frac{1}{3}v\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281359 1072 #uuid "92a30208-5e48-56e6-a327-0e416b1fdedd" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281359 1074 "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} \\\\ u-\\frac{1}{3}v\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3{u}^{2} & -v \\\\ 1 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(3{u}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})-(1)\\cdot (-v) \\\\  & =v-{u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v-{u}^{2} & <0 \\\\ v & <{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv={u}^{2}\n$$\n\ndefines a parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,-1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/13/1368ee41963c1abcc320d2cd35449c08599582f37c7d68f750e137fcdcd2e57b.png)" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281360 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281187 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281360 1069 "q-161843" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281360 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281360 1071 "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}4u+{v}^{2} \\\\ v-{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281360 1072 #uuid "553ab218-87bf-5c6a-b2ae-3d3c054a906a" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281360 1074 "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}4u+{v}^{2} \\\\ v-{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2v \\\\ -2u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1-(-2u)\\cdot (2v) \\\\  & =4+4uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4+4uv & >0 \\\\ 4uv & >-4 \\\\ uv & >-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the solution $v>-\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u>0$ and $v<-\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u<0.$\n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=-\\frac{1}{u}\n$$\n\ndefines a hyperbola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n  \n\n![](images/c5/c501e7e341af57e86f18d73871314800d453f79572e16ccf2477335352d4dcdc.png)" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281361 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280893 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281361 1069 "q-161844" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281361 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281361 1071 "![](images/3e/3e28d562bb7edd9a067bb3ee2c7cae07dc3b76315f39eeed084bdb06682042a2.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=6x,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281361 1072 #uuid "0bfb6a93-e576-5e3e-ab7f-e68f79fe16d1" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281361 1074 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $3$ centered at $(3,0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=6x$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =6x \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =6\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/70/70450f3fc9e9971a76e4458c5e020c1f264e739913fbb24d3e6ff22359542316.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281362 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280915 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281362 1069 "q-161845" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281362 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281362 1071 "Let $f:U\\to R$ be continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R$ such that $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U.$ Which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- The function $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a.$\n- The inverse ${f}^{-1}$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281362 1072 #uuid "8b9d32a6-dbbc-54ba-9b9d-46f810008dd3" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281362 1074 "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the theorem, the function $f$ must be invertible in some neighborhood of $a.$\n\n- Statement II is also true. Indeed, the inverse ${f}^{-1}$ must be continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n\n- Statement III is also true. According to the theorem, we must have ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281363 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280886 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281363 1069 "q-161850" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281363 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281363 1071 "![](images/dc/dcd901b032075a4c4a22c991d9bb3e5e5ff49e7a2c91e7e840ede1516085f683.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2y,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281363 1072 #uuid "5c8589a5-fbbf-5ba9-ada4-6032f911a838" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281363 1074 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $1$ centered at $(0,1,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2y$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2y \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =2\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/73/736c46d0121c646def56c6b8fede81a4155fffb97032facd75a6cdf29f98f65a.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281364 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280978 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281364 1069 "q-161853" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281364 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281364 1071 "![](images/a1/a1e4a1e39c85a037f3f4f49910b651300363ef34bee969464f5e711aa00986e3.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}4{x}^{2}z\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the right one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281364 1072 #uuid "2dd43989-4160-5cb0-b938-4fe05b8b6278" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281364 1074 "![](images/91/9165163be9ce95e7165f04d26e6ce8080699ab4d477eba3f804c849fdf1fb679.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type III region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-z},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/ca/ca74bf149c1371fe9f1622586d9eb92fe180a9c9bb6a0c53d16f538b3c55f4a4.png)\n\nHence, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-z}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}4{x}^{2}z\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4-z}4{x}^{2}z\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4-z}4{x}^{2}z\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-z}4{x}^{2}z\\,dy]dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}4{x}^{2}z[y]_{y=0}^{y=4-z}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}4{x}^{2}z(4-z)\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(16{x}^{2}z-4{x}^{2}{z}^{2})\\,dz\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(16{x}^{2}z-4{x}^{2}{z}^{2})\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{3}(16{x}^{2}z-4{x}^{2}{z}^{2})\\,dz]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[8{x}^{2}{z}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{2}{z}^{3}]_{z=0}^{z=3}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}36{x}^{2}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}36{x}^{2}\\,dx & ={[12{x}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =(12-0) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281365 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281043 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281365 1069 "q-161854" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281365 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281365 1071 "![](images/69/693290f788fac9ba63ef072c1d76a9c6bb8fce9cafbaeada5abb425262347186.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=8z,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281365 1072 #uuid "7b1ff4bb-9996-5474-b649-ae1b809bddb5" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281365 1074 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $4$ centered at $(0,0,4).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=8z$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =8z \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =8\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -8\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/f5/f508e47498e6fa625fef03bf42051410362763ee0b0731c776d207ca8647559f.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,8\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281366 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280906 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281366 1069 "q-161855" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281366 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281366 1071 "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{uv} \\\\ {u}^{2}+v\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281366 1072 #uuid "ee68f9ff-69b4-5317-9406-24330023ed30" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281366 1074 "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{uv} \\\\ {u}^{2}+v\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v{e}^{uv} & u{e}^{uv} \\\\ 2u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v{e}^{uv}\\cdot (1)-(2u)\\cdot u{e}^{uv} \\\\  & ={e}^{uv}(v-2{u}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{uv}(v-2{u}^{2}) & >0 \\\\ v-2{u}^{2} & >0 \\\\ v & >2{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=2{u}^{2}\n$$\n\ndefines a parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(0,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/61/613e83ce5ad6154905ec9001ece078309e396561e251e85455ff261bb4cd61a0.png)" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281367 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280933 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281367 1069 "q-161857" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281367 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281367 1071 "![](images/0a/0a79260df29825684d6bdf6197df8581adf5a5a2d1d89938c299f18b3f33bf7c.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=2$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=3.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=1$\n- ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=-2$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=2.91$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)}$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281367 1072 #uuid "4eb620b0-5fb5-5c19-a1ea-9934c615ac57" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281367 1074 "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=1.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=1,$ for example, $(0,2),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=1,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(1)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, $f$ is invertible in *some* neighborhood of $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that $y=-2=f(2).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=2$ since ${f}^{′}(2)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $y=2.91=f(3),$ but $f$ is not continuous at $x=3.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281368 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281092 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281368 1069 "q-161858" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281368 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281368 1071 "![](images/54/540e1fac13ed45d6db5c857678a7ccf410b3834213adb2b984cb6f942d4dba2f.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=-2$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=-3.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=-3$\n- ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=2$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=4$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)}$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281368 1072 #uuid "649157e5-32cb-5583-98fc-af0b21c39379" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281368 1074 "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that $f$ is not continuous at $x=-3.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement II is also false. Notice that $y=2=f(-2).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=-2$ since ${f}^{′}(-2)=0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that $y=4=f(0),$ and the function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x=0.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=0,$ for example, $(-0.1,0.1),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x=0,$ i.e., ${f}^{′}(0)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, the derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=4$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281186 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281369 1069 "q-161865" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281369 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281369 1071 "![](images/aa/aa872c37fc7912e4c246c80c391ee37ae57f23f6c1010681c367ce2ec6d901c2.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=3$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=0.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=1$\n- ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=-0.88$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=2.5$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}