eating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(4x-xz)\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{3}(4x-xz)\\,dz]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[4xz-\\frac{1}{2}x{z}^{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=3}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{15}{2}x\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{15}{2}x\\,dx & ={[\\frac{15}{4}{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =15-0 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281372 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280875 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281372 1069 "q-161873" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281372 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281372 1071 "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(2,2)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{u}-v \\\\ {u}^{2}v\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281372 1072 #uuid "9ee4cd0d-00ad-5fb0-be36-91eca3f6f7a9" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281372 1074 "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}} & -1 \\\\ 2uv & {u}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}})\\cdot ({u}^{2})-2uv\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-1+2uv \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(2,2)} & =(-1+2uv)\\,{|}_{u=2\\,v=2} \\\\  & =-1+2(2)(2) \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|7|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =7\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281373 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280943 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281373 1069 "q-161874" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281373 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281373 1071 "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(4,2)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{u}+{v}^{2} \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281373 1072 #uuid "9201622d-d447-58b4-8006-2fbea11393d4" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281373 1074 "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}} & 2v \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot u-v\\cdot 2v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{u}-2{v}^{2} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(4,2)} & ={\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{u}-2{v}^{2}\\,|}_{u=4\\,v=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4}-2(2)^{2} \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-7|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =7\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281374 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280929 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281374 1069 "q-161875" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281374 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281374 1071 "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(0,3)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u-{v}^{2} \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281374 1072 #uuid "8a571cf4-d621-5149-95d3-896e62b990d5" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281374 1074 "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & -2v \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot (-2v) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+2v \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(0,3)} & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+2v)\\,{|}_{u=0\\,v=3} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+2(3) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|6|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =6\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281243 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281375 1069 "q-161882" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281375 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281375 1071 "![](images/cc/cc5c179ec93879776b7f033f8a62674fcf272ca0b1270cef0902905192d613f4.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the half-sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+5z=0$ for $y\\ge 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281375 1072 #uuid "701f6c59-3d11-55f5-b63f-3f32b6e78e41" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281375 1074 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-half of the sphere of radius $\\frac{5}{2}$ centered at $(0,0,-\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+5z=0$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+5z & =0 \\\\ {\\rho }^{2}+5\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ & =0 \\\\ \\rho (\\rho +5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/22/22924aa3079443eb0c712afbb80bbe1b58407cec297a8ef4c90fb0e69aec9735.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -5\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281183 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281376 1069 "q-161883" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281376 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281376 1071 "![](images/a3/a32e8fd24c990a831cfee9525c6b1c035fb4ee6e09deedb3ac4c899d90ceeaac.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the half-sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4y=0$ for $z\\le 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281376 1072 #uuid "5c94b1ab-44af-5e40-9fea-2f1ea696e7ba" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281376 1074 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-half of the sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(0,-2,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4y=0$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4y & =0 \\\\ {\\rho }^{2}+4\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & =0 \\\\ \\rho (\\rho +4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/e2/e2865e838521a8864ad76d72e520d4b128a8fceb8c18cb9c35ba10134419c4eb.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281377 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281203 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281377 1069 "q-161885" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281377 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281377 1071 "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(2,4)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{u}^{2}}{v} \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281377 1072 #uuid "4a48d509-ce9f-57c3-ab71-29eb65410f8f" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281377 1074 "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2u}{v} & -\\frac{{u}^{2}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2u}{v}\\cdot u-v\\cdot (-\\frac{{u}^{2}}{{v}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2{u}^{2}}{v}+\\frac{{u}^{2}}{v} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{u}^{2}}{v} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(2,4)} & =(\\frac{3{u}^{2}}{v})\\,{|}_{u=2\\,v=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(2)^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|3|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =3\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281378 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281075 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281378 1069 "q-161887" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281378 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281378 1071 "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(1,4)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{uv} \\\\ u{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281378 1072 #uuid "787ef4d5-9dfe-5be7-87e1-4a46bed285bf" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281378 1074 "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{v}}{2\\sqrt{u}} & \\frac{\\sqrt{u}}{2\\sqrt{v}} \\\\ {v}^{2} & 2uv\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{v}}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 2uv-{v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{u}}{2\\sqrt{v}} \\\\  & =v\\sqrt{uv}-\\frac{1}{2}v\\sqrt{uv} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}v\\sqrt{uv} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,4)} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}v\\sqrt{uv})\\,|}_{u=1\\,v=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4)\\sqrt{(1)(4)} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|4|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =4\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281379 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280892 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281379 1069 "q-161888" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281379 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281379 1071 "![](images/f6/f65b6429452cd985e064734d6e7e62c63abb58e2e481e5b4781635be8d4f1d8e.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the quarter-sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+3x=0$ for $y\\le 0$ and $z\\ge 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281379 1072 #uuid "c9e85e76-c9ab-5f95-ac8b-90b2e67e565a" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281379 1074 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $\\frac{3}{2}$ centered at $(-\\frac{3}{2},0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+3x=0$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ {\\rho }^{2}+3\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/7d/7d161ad5adc8f99f81634049c487f299f84edde255b2f08aa3d1dd4060f99e75.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281380 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281111 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281380 1069 "q-161895" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281380 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281380 1071 "![](images/0e/0e374f18d8b87de2fddd9a2127ee84709746c7c1b75083441afcc7b6c79402fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨-z,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281380 1072 #uuid "fca4156b-f8d5-50fa-a7b9-3894a3eed862" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281380 1074 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper paraboloid ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=-z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5,\\,Q=0,\\,R=x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)\\cdot (-2x)-0\\cdot (-2y)+x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x+x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-9x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨-z,0,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-9x)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281381 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280865 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281381 1069 "q-161899" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281381 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281381 1071 "![](images/8a/8a48be0b4c101babf157a05959e923576ad5614113b3eec9e93fe747d5dc3803.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,x,\\,0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281381 1072 #uuid "a4e1f048-4c99-5435-999b-508c95c11d59" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281381 1074 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper hemisphere ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=x,\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-1\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})-x\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})+0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨1,x,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A+0 \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281382 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281076 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281382 1069 "q-161905" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281382 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281382 1071 "Consider the vector field $F=⟨z,\\,0,\\,y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5$ and the plane $z=0.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281382 1072 #uuid "ca856459-2199-5fb8-97c9-ba0974b5b2db" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281382 1074 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$\n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨z,0,y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5,\\,Q=0,\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)\\cdot (2x)-(0)\\cdot (2y)+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+10x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x-y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x-y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281383 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280961 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281383 1069 "q-16191" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281383 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281383 1071 "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,\\,\\,y=t(5-t),\\,0\\le t\\le 5\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281383 1072 #uuid "8129c06e-0ff8-5227-9bfc-870198aba1b0" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281383 1074 "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x={t}^{2}.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ | $16$ | $25$ |\n| $y$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t(5-t).$ This gives\n\n| **