e partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4)=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4)=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=-y,\\,Q=z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4,\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-(-y)\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4)\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-y+\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y-\\frac{xy+4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & =0+{∬}_{D}y-\\frac{xy+4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y-\\frac{xy+4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281385 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281009 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281385 1069 "q-161911" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281385 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281385 1071 "![](images/57/5709af87720dcb052d8ffc6cc600c5889e45849aee6bed69ec3646e43c3cefb1.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨z,\\,1,\\,0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281385 1072 #uuid "2fe585d2-13a2-5e4d-ba70-80b8560f5587" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281385 1074 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨z,1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3,\\,Q=1,\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3)\\cdot 2x-1\\cdot 2y+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+6x-2y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-6x+2y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & =0+{∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-6x+2y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-6x+2y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281386 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281097 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281386 1069 "q-161914" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281386 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281386 1071 "Consider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,z,\\,y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and the hemisphere $z=\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281386 1072 #uuid "82300a07-b333-5d6e-9b87-61998889d7c6" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281386 1074 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper hemisphere ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=z=\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}},\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})-\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(0+y+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (2x)-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot (2y)+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(0-2y{x}^{2}-2{y}^{3}+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2y{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}-y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}2y{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}-y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2y{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}+y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281387 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281287 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281387 1069 "q-161915" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281387 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281387 1071 "Consider the vector field $F=⟨y,\\,0,\\,-z⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloids $z=1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281387 1072 #uuid "5b046a51-1f6d-54c9-b348-4c228eae458f" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281387 1074 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper paraboloid ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=y,\\,Q=0,\\,R=-z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (-2x)-(0)\\cdot (-2y)+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=y,\\,Q=0,\\,R=-z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (2x)-(0)\\cdot (2y)+4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-2xy+4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4xy+2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-5\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281388 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281135 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281388 1069 "q-16192" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281388 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281388 1071 "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\nx=3\\sqrt{t},\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{3}-2t,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281388 1072 #uuid "7277827b-15b7-554d-a387-f11139dd3ae6" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281388 1074 "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=3\\sqrt{t}.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.75$ | $1$ | $1.25$ | $1.5$ | $1.75$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1.5$ | $2.12$ | $2.60$ | $3$ | $3.35$ | $3.67$ | $3.97$ | $4.24$ |\n| $y$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y={t}^{3}-2t.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.75$ | $1$ | $1.25$ | $1.5$ | $1.75$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1.5$ | $2.12$ | $2.60$ | $3$ | $3.35$ | $3.67$ | $3.97$ | $4.24$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $-0.48$ | $-0.88$ | $-1.08$ | $-1$ | $-0.55$ | $0.38$ | $1.86$ | $4$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/88/8897c9d1d91007a51d715d982e6b1137bdc5f83f6ef89802b4115522809d4db5.png)" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281389 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281200 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281389 1069 "q-161930" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281389 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281389 1071 "![](images/93/93df9649232a5985eaefebd7180c8f40c6608d51fe8b993cf3da9a8473eb4612.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}xz\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the right one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281389 1072 #uuid "ba39098f-435f-58cd-84ca-019d2ea965a9" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281389 1074 "![](images/b4/b4428666d80d78dfa3a4e754df7e5b33ee7ee4a8712c617b4cef5400a9ba494d.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type III region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2-\\frac{x}{8}-z},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/61/61de9aa1f6230dafb68c99d1b6345ee49c30a6d2f3f4825e44e6032daac23335.png)\n\nHence, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2-\\frac{x}{8}-z}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}xz\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x/8-z}xz\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x/8-z}xz\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-x/8-z}xz\\,dy]dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xz[y]_{y=0}^{y=2-x/8-z}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xz(2-\\frac{x}{8}-z)\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2xz-\\frac{{x}^{2}z}{8}-x{z}^{2})\\,dz\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2xz-\\frac{{x}^{2}z}{8}-x{z}^{2})\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2xz-\\frac{{x}^{2}z}{8}-x{z}^{2})\\,dz]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{[x{z}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}{z}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{x{z}^{3}}{3}]}_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{x}{3})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16})\\,dx & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{48}]}_{x=0}^{x=4} \\\\  & =(\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{64}{48})-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281390 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281136 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281390 1069 "q-16198" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281390 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281390 1071 "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281390 1072 #uuid "76a9a95a-1f50-593a-bd3b-81528897e580" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281390 1074 "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$ This gives\n\n| **$\\theta $** | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ | $\\pi $ | $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $7$ | $\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $-7$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{2}$ |\n| $y$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$ This gives\n\n| **$\\theta $** | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ | $\\pi $ | $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $7$ | $\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $-7$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{2}$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $-\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/de/debf9475589e7e242df986d1fffb8a97834d6ce550279fc82b0428001b661eaa.png)" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281391 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281162 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281391 1069 "q-16199" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281391 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281391 1071 "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-2t,\\,\\,\\,y=3-t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\ndetermine the range of $x.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281391 1072 #uuid "97007632-9d2f-5a8a-900e-9e7bf33ec6d3" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281391 1074 "To find the range of $x,$ we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2}-2t \\\\  & ={(t-1)}^{2}-{(1)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(t-1)}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${(t-1)}^{2}\\ge 0,$ we can conclude that $x\\ge -1.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280953 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281392 1069 "q-16200" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281392 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281392 1071 "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=5t-{t}^{2},\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281392 1072 #uuid "2dc3a2b7-620d-5940-a1aa-2a26502f43cc" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281392 1074 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5t-{t}^{2} \\\\  & =-({t}^{2}-5t) \\\\  & =-{(t-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =-{(t-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{25}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${(t-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}\\ge 0,$ we can conclude that $x\\le \\frac{25}{4}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that ${t}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Consequently, $y\\ge 0.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $x\\le \\frac{25}{4}$ and $y\\ge 0.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281393 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280888 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281393 1069 "q-162147" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281393 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281393 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,z,\\,y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=\\frac{1}{2}x-y-1$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 3.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\frac{3}{2}.$*" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281393 1072 #uuid "0d7b3552-b54a-5c05-93a2-3660e2678f53" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281393 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=\\frac{1}{2}x-y-1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=-1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=1.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-1,\\,2,\\,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=\\frac{1}{2}x-y-1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,z,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-1,\\,2,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,\\,\\frac{1}{2}x-y-1,\\,y⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,2,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-x+(x-2y-2)+2y) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-\\frac{2}{3})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{\\cancel{2}}{\\cancel{3}})\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{3}}{\\cancel{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281394 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280936 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281394 1069 "q-162154" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281394 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281394 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x+3,\\,x+y,\\,z-x+6⟩$ and a finite region $S$ of the plane $z=x-y$ such that $\\text{Area}(S)=\\sqrt{6}.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281394 1072 #uuid "88097b92-017e-5e6e-bb41-84bae76dd7ac" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281394 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x-y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x-y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x+3,\\,x+y,\\,z-x+6⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨x+3,\\,x+y,\\,6-y⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(-(x+3)+(x+y)+(6-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{6} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281019 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281395 1069 "q-162161" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281395 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281395 1071 "The equation below describes all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(3,0,-2)$ and $Q(1,1,-1).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ b \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281395 1072 #uuid "d31dc5a7-37f0-52db-ac08-e744b2aa0398" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281395 1074 "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand the vector equation of the  line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=3+1+0=4.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281396 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280948 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281396 1069 "q-162165" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281396 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281396 1071 "The equation below describes all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(0,-2,2)$ and $Q(1,-1,1).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ b \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281396 1072 #uuid "c6ef52c7-4e9c-5db7-b87c-ad4749b7588e" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281396 1074 "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand the vector equation of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2+1+0=3.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281397 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280934 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281397 1069 "q-162167" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281397 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281397 1071 "The equation below describes all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(-3,5,0)$ and $Q(0,-3,2).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -8 \\\\ b\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281397 1072 #uuid "6982b399-45f0-5a83-aeee-4f0df799362d" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281397 1074 "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand the vector equation of the  line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=(-3)+2+0=-1.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281398 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281214 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281398 1069 "q-162168" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281398 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281398 1071 "The equation below describes all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(-6,2,-1)$ and $Q(-8,0,-1).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ b \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,c]\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281398 1072 #uuid "3bcbe167-633d-5f59-83f8-d10ba4510f50" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281398 1074 "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand the vector equation of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=(-1)+(-2)+1=-2.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281399 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281081 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281399 1069 "q-162169" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281399 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281399 1071 "The equation below describes all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(5,0,4)$ and $Q(-3,1,5).