overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(j-3\\,k)-(3\\,i+2\\,j-k) \\\\  & =-3\\,i-j-2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=(3\\,i+2\\,j-k)+t(-3\\,i-j-2\\,k)\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(3\\,i+2\\,j-k)+t(-3\\,i-j-2\\,k) \\\\  & =(3-3t)\\,i+(2-t)\\,j-(2t+1)\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=(3-3t)+(2-t)+1=6-4t.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281401 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280905 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281401 1069 "q-162177" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281401 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281401 1071 "The equation below gives a parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(-1,1,3)$ and $Q(0,6,-1).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=a\\,i+(5t+1)\\,j+b\\,k,\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b+c?$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281401 1072 #uuid "910d640d-077f-59e7-bef4-a838dd071664" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281401 1074 "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(6\\,j-k)-(-i+j+3\\,k) \\\\  & =i+5\\,j-4\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=(-i+j+3\\,k)+t(i+5\\,j-4\\,k)\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(-i+j+3\\,k)+t(i+5\\,j-4\\,k) \\\\  & =(t-1)\\,i+(5t+1)\\,j+(3-4t)\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=(t-1)+(3-4t)+0=2-3t.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281402 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280965 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281402 1069 "q-162178" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281402 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281402 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281402 1072 #uuid "644f8abf-d61c-5475-b3d7-b08f23c7f271" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281402 1074 "The matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=0\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4=-4.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|-4|=4.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281403 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281211 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281403 1069 "q-162182" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281403 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281403 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281403 1072 #uuid "02e4a694-0c9e-57f7-ab0e-3cb84ca52e57" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281403 1074 "The matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}|=(-1)\\cdot (-3)-(-2)\\cdot (-2)=-1.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|-1|=1.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281404 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280945 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281404 1069 "q-162184" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281404 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281404 1071 "The equation below gives a parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(-4,0,-3)$ and $Q(-6,2,-5).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=-a\\,i+2t\\,j-b\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,c]\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b+c?$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281404 1072 #uuid "1fd0bbbd-dfcf-55de-83d3-53fd95d405bf" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281404 1074 "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(-6\\,i+2\\,j-5\\,k)-(-4\\,i-3\\,k) \\\\  & =-2\\,i+2\\,j-2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=(-4\\,i-3\\,k)+t(-2\\,i+2\\,j-2\\,k)\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(-4\\,i-3\\,k)+t(-2\\,i+2\\,j-2\\,k) \\\\  & =-(2t+4)\\,i+2t\\,j-(2t+3)\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=(2t+4)+(2t+3)+1=4t+8.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281279 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281405 1069 "q-162188" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281405 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281405 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=u+v-2,\\,y=u-3v+1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281405 1072 #uuid "a00aae13-9eba-5781-8e1a-54e7168af3f9" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281405 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u+v-2 \\\\ u-3v+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u+v \\\\ u-3v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot (-3)-1\\cdot 1=-4.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|-4|=4.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281406 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280931 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281406 1069 "q-162189" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281406 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281406 1071 "The equation below gives a parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(1,-1,1)$ and $Q(2,-3,0).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=a\\,i-(2t+1)\\,j+b\\,k,\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b+c?$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281406 1072 #uuid "4c5f20af-533a-5761-9687-aadcbb2c7fbc" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281406 1074 "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(2\\,i-3\\,j)-(i-j+k) \\\\  & =i-2\\,j-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=(i-j+k)+t(i-2\\,j-k)\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(i-j+k)+t(i-2\\,j-k) \\\\  & =(t+1)\\,i-(2t+1)\\,j+(1-t)\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=(t+1)+(1-t)+0=2.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281407 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280864 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281407 1069 "q-162190" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281407 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281407 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=2u-v,\\,y=2v-u-5.\n$$\n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281407 1072 #uuid "cf48a6fd-c438-5f8f-9914-46d60d716c8e" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281407 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-v \\\\ 2v-u-5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-v \\\\ -u+2v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=2\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-1)=3.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|3|=3.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281408 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281088 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281408 1069 "q-162191" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281408 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281408 1071 "The equation below gives a parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(4,3,5)$ and $Q(6,3,1).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=(2t+4)\\,i+a\\,j+b\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,c]\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b+c?$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281408 1072 #uuid "3bc3398e-446c-5c75-b6ec-3e2bf640f0db" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281408 1074 "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(6\\,i+3\\,j+k)-(4\\,i+3\\,j+5\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\,i-4\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=(4\\,i+3\\,j+5\\,k)+t(2\\,i-4\\,k)\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(4\\,i+3\\,j+5\\,k)+t(2\\,i-4\\,k) \\\\  & =(2t+4)\\,i+3\\,j+(5-4t)\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=3+(5-4t)+1=9-4t.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281409 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280973 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281409 1069 "q-162193" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281409 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281409 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=u+5v+2,\\,y=1-u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281409 1072 #uuid "faa815d3-1a15-5270-95c0-06e76987590b" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281409 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u+5v+2 \\\\ 1-u\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u+5v \\\\ -u\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 5=5.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|5|=5.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281410 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280966 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281410 1069 "q-162195" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281410 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281410 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=2u-2v-3,\\,y=u-v+2.\n$$\n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281410 1072 #uuid "79b6b1e2-201b-5b88-aae8-abebb7edf5f4" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281410 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-2v-3 \\\\ u-v+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-2v \\\\ u-v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|=2\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot (-2)=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|det(A)|=|0|=0.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281411 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281100 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281411 1069 "q-162196" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281411 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281411 1071 "![](images/57/576d60f179b56159504efc59178ada3db798046d01a7dbc92986cd1162f770ea.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nx=u+2v+1,\\,y=2u+v-1.\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the square shown above under the action of $T?$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281411 1072 #uuid "7aaa6c3c-ccc5-5e4c-bbad-d399e1496037" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281411 1074 "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nThe given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u+2v+1 \\\\ 2u+v-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u+2v \\\\ 2u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a non-singular matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is invertible and the image of the square under the action of $T$ is a parallelogram." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281412 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281276 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281412 1069 "q-162199" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281412 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281412 1071 "![](images/eb/eb1247ddcaaf12a342aa036550ed4f9f7daaeb6037e7ebbab0de619bb6975dbe.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the pentagon shown above under the action of $T?$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281412 1072 #uuid "0a4d15c5-f7e2-5db8-ae64-0fe650e3a234" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281412 1074 "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281413 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280852 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281413 1069 "q-162200" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281413 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281413 1071 "![](images/da/da6c0fb32e62b6effe133c527fee528c47b6f1c8dc7dd1d77d7d04f42b4c3d2b.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T?$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281413 1072 #uuid "d640e1ef-8b1e-5a87-a6d1-5fc3bd1f3743" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281413 1074 "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [1759218628141