.\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281415 1072 #uuid "b8f12922-35f6-5a3b-8472-719f4cc88613" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281415 1074 "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-8)-4\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. Therefore, the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a line segment." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281416 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280951 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281416 1069 "q-162203" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281416 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281416 1071 "![](images/bf/bfbd80c4e052ae3c286d25b2cd7bf5a1ffcd76ac5a030b7f9b633d0f12517231.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the pentagon shown above under the action of $T?$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281416 1072 #uuid "d076341b-6900-5a76-9cb3-7d856b14ee1