ibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 6-(-4)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. Therefore, the image of the quadrilateral under the action of $T$ is a line segment." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281418 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280863 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281418 1069 "q-162207" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281418 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281418 1071 "![](images/57/57948af956c9b83ed944fafc26e81a5c003401f753bc520e088ffdbd71915c3a.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281418 1072 #uuid "cd1aad0c-e4b0-51b3-9d8d-afbb5058a343" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281418 1074 "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n