\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the pentagon shown above under the action of $T?$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281426 1072 #uuid "4197fe63-5776-5546-b6a0-81601947295c" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281426 1074 "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-4)-(-2)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible, and the image of the pentagon under the action of $T$ is a line segment." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281427 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281205 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281427 1069 "q-162230" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281427 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281427 1071 "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i-x\\,j+z\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k$ for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ).$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281427 1072 #uuid "fd877a62-b477-55ef-9e5a-194da8c5c887" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281427 1074 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,z={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i-x\\,j+z\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j+2t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j+2t\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+{t}^{2}\\cdot 2t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+2{t}^{3} \\\\  & =1+2{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(1+2{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[t+\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =((\\pi -(-\\pi ))+\\frac{{\\pi }^{4}-(-\\pi )^{4}}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281428 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281251 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281428 1069 "q-162235" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281428 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281428 1071 "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=(x+y)\\,i-z\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=(1-{t}^{2})\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281428 1072 #uuid "13da6f40-f5bf-549b-a97f-20a44b83344f" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281428 1074 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=1-{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(x+y)\\,i-z\\,k \\\\  & =(1-{t}^{2}+{t}^{2})\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k \\\\  & =i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =-2t\\,i+2t\\,j+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k)\\cdot (-2t\\,i+2t\\,j+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2t)+0\\cdot 2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =-2t+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-2t+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-{t}^{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)}{4}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-1-\\frac{1}{4})-(-1-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281429 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281169 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281429 1069 "q-162236" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281429 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281429 1071 "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{xy}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$*" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281429 1072 #uuid "871a8fcb-2fd3-5ed7-9580-a8fb144d6d26" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281429 1074 "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,x\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le xy}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{xy}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/6b/6b3720af66e81aef6792d4b2a34b2bdf2b93bd59ed6e28e4b38a1e06964bb2cc.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type I region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x\\le y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nx=0\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=x\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le y},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/9d/9dc99cc0df9f089b9f62402c3c5ea9c4408be077debbf6dfadeb884ee4d8b2d0.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{xy}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{\\int }_{0}^{xy}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{xy}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})[z]_{0}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})[{\\int }_{0}^{y}2x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})[{x}^{2}]_{0}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{4})]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280990 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281430 1069 "q-162237" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281430 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281430 1071 "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=-z\\,j+y\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281430 1072 #uuid "b7cf2656-c8b3-55d8-81c3-17aef82ea92a" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281430 1074 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =-z\\,j+y\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k) \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t. \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[t]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281431 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280877 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281431 1069 "q-162245" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281431 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281431 1071 "Which of the following diagrams shows all the $9$th roots of unity?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281431 1072 #uuid "983bb684-2ee0-5f31-84ff-a2abe736945d" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281431 1074 "Geometrically, the $9$th roots of unity have the following properties:\n\n- all of them lie on the unit circle centered in the origin,\n\n- one of the roots is $1,$ corresponding to the point $(1,0)$ on the complex plane,\n\n- the roots form a regular nonagon (regular $9$-sided polygon).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/d8/d8a917138beb862f6816fc059847659b1ba11e17d6a7a4906309b021baf1d3e5.png)" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281432 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281163 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281432 1069 "q-162246" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281432 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281432 1071 "Which of the following diagrams shows all the $10$th roots of unity?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281432 1072 #uuid "2fb37d25-f0d5-5d55-9af1-e862b4c21453" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281432 1074 "Geometrically, the $10$th roots of unity have the following properties:\n\n- all of them lie on the unit circle centered in the origin,\n\n- one of the roots is $1,$ corresponding to the point $(1,0)$ on the complex plane,\n\n- the roots form a regular decagon (regular $10$-sided polygon).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/82/82682434de1fe7e9c7f086334f0ef7e0a7b2fde3d64efd7234ea10f1fb6c3b4f.png)" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281433 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281132 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281433 1069 "q-162247" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281433 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281433 1071 "By changing the order of integration in the following repeated integrals so that they are integrated first with respect to $z,$ then $x,$ and then $y,$ express the following sum as a single repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{-2{x}^{3}}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2x}}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281433 1072 #uuid "14061a2e-2a76-5b88-b932-249bcad9073d" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281433 1074 "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integrals, we see that the domain of integration $R={R}_{1}\\cup {R}_{2},$ where ${R}_{1}$ and ${R}_{2}$ are type I regions, is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,\\,-2{x}^{3}\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,-y\\le z\\le x+3}, \\\\ {R}_{2} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,\\sqrt{2x}\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,-y\\le z\\le x+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/83/834238127fb9a55c956b2dd708e1dc7d925c63725990003eff6aac28ee42a873.png)\n\nThe region $D$ consists of two type I regions in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,-2{x}^{3}\\le y\\le 2}\\,\\cup \\,{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\sqrt{2x}\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=-2{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,x=-\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y}{2}}\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{2x}\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,-\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y}{2}}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/82/82bddc0c83e0e5a5824b7525bcd4fd2dc9b8f88473dfe1fd5c6cdb2ff0cefcaf.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt[3]{y/2}}^{{y}^{2}/2}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281434 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281113 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281434 1069 "q-162249" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281434 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281434 1071 "By changing the order of integration in the following repeated integrals so that they are integrated first with respect to $x,$ then $y,$ and then $z,$ express the following sum as a single repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y+2}}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{2}^{6}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{6-y}}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281434 1072 #uuid "9c5dd846-0a3a-5a82-a678-28ac12606de8" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281434 1074 "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integrals, we see that the domain of integration $R={R}_{1}\\cup {R}_{2},$ where ${R}_{1}$ and ${R}_{2}$ are type I regions, is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-2\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{y+2},\\,\\,-z\\le x\\le {y}^{2}}, \\\\ {R}_{2} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,2\\le y\\le 6,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{6-y},\\,\\,-z\\le x\\le {y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/aa/aa1c884900c3cb01a719dc6d22604469984d2b08291d9d925b8f37d794d455b2.png)\n\nThe region $D$ consists of two type I regions in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,-2\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{y+2}}\\,\\cup \\,{(y,z)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 6,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{6-y}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{y+2}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{6-y}\\,⟹\\,y=6-{z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,{z}^{2}-2\\le y\\le 6-{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4139dde4d56e83b91e4a72d42ccdf296ad20773520ccdaa51d7a84b9e1a3f31.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-2}^{6-{z}^{2}}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281435 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281000 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281435 1069 "q-162250" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281435 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281435 1071 "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$*" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281435 1072 #uuid "90d38ce0-881b-51da-a009-3b19a6113351" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281435 1074 "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le {e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/38/38eb7316b1ee5c947eee37a9cc10530ff08f793b7564cf0d88c6ce24e1239f9d.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type II region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y\\,⟹\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/24/241abff7fae5d6ba3301d605ff4c4e62ff61e423515c7bc3a54b17b9aec525aa.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}[{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}[z]_{0}^{{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}[y]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-({e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi /2}-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}) \\\\  & =-(1-e) \\\\  & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281436 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281104 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281436 1069 "q-162251" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281436 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281436 1071 "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$*" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281436 1072 #uuid "716050fb-6be1-5474-89de-ead930408990" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281436 1074 "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}},\\,\\,x\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le {y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/62/62377f3e744c5767927f9591b8c6e3335a8036cad5fe9c62f52c041b2db2bac8.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type I region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}},\\,x\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nx=0\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=x\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}},\\,0\\le x\\le y},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/05/05de07930f6cacfd0a3a21045a95628891965bb69d5df1bef4dbeeab9e480b97.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{y}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡z]_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})[{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})[x]_{0}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281437 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281281 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281437 1069 "q-16227" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281437 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281437 1071 "What is the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=1?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281437 1072 #uuid "d9834690-2d79-5a22-9e52-3ff6dc17940f" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281437 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nThe sine function has a period of $2\\pi .$ Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi .\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281438 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281201 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281438 1069 "q-16228" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281438 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281438 1071 "What is the general solution to the equation  $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}?$  Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281438 1072 #uuid "7f222b3a-eb4c-598d-98b0-57764691bb32" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281438 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](images/bf/bf1efb64724fb0dfbf60cc14905e63ba867caa0bf0edd888b35fb554af97c856.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi .\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281439 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280946 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281439 1069 "q-162307" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281439 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281439 1071 "Which of the following diagrams shows all the $5$th roots of unity?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281439 1072 #uuid "24f981b3-ee5c-5f1a-a228-a6d47937825c" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281439 1074 "Geometrically, the $5$th roots of unity have the following properties:\n\n- all of them lie on the unit circle centered in the origin,\n\n- one of the roots is $1,$ corresponding to the point $(1,0)$ on the complex plane,\n\n- the roots form a regular pentagon (regular $5$-sided polygon).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/bf/bf2edb811442fc770f6f3c119c06bca3c17d30537f1941448ddb6ab0afa3eb8a.png)" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281440 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281065 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281440 1069 "q-162312" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281440 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281440 1071 "Which of the following diagrams shows all the $10$th roots of unity?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281440 1072 #uuid "b8bcfe69-77ce-592b-889b-3f4e303f93d1" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281440 1074 "Geometrically, the $10$th roots of unity have the following properties:\n\n- all of them lie on the unit circle centered in the origin,\n\n- one of the roots is $1,$ corresponding to the point $(1,0)$ on the complex plane,\n\n- the roots form a regular decagon (regular $10$-sided polygon).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/82/82682434de1fe7e9c7f086334f0ef7e0a7b2fde3d64efd7234ea10f1fb6c3b4f.png)" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281441 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281131 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281441 1069 "q-162313" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281441 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281441 1071 "Which of the following diagrams shows all the $6$th roots of unity?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281441 1072 #uuid "c6043afc-edfe-5093-a233-415cfa3b2c2b" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281441 1074 "Geometrically, the $6$th roots of unity have the following properties:\n\n- all of them lie on the unit circle centered in the origin,\n\n- one of the roots is $1,$ corresponding to the point $(1,0)$ on the complex plane,\n\n- the roots form a regular hexagon (regular $6$-sided polygon).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/92/923d1a1b87833551ae6c451f15a6b5d8bdd75623e8d5c20e86357894bc24683d.png)" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281442 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280982 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281442 1069 "q-162316" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281442 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281442 1071 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\,j+({t}^{3}-t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,0).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=z\\,i+y\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281442 1072 #uuid "8e15967c-9185-5388-a8b6-baaf9a1de47a" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281442 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{3}-2t,\\,y=3{t}^{2}+3t,\\,z={t}^{3}-t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =z\\,i+x\\,k \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-t)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\,j-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-t)\\,k \\\\  & =(6{t}^{2}-2)\\,i+(6t+3)\\,j+(3{t}^{2}-1)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(({t}^{3}-t)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\,k)\\cdot ((6{t}^{2}-2)\\,i+(6t+3)\\,j+(3{t}^{2}-1)\\,k) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}-t)\\cdot (6{t}^{2}-2)+0\\cdot (6t+3)+(3{t}^{2}+3t)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =6{t}^{5}+9{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(6{t}^{5}+9{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[{t}^{6}+\\frac{9{t}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}-{t}^{3}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(1-\\frac{9}{5}+\\frac{1}{4}+1-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281443 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280868 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281443 1069 "q-162323" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281443 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281443 1071 "By changing the order of integration, find the missing limits in the following repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{(x+1)^{3}}^{2-{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{x+z}^{z+2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{p}{\\int }_{q}^{\\sqrt[3]{z}-1}{\\int }_{x+z}^{z+2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{r}^{0}{\\int }_{x+z}^{z+2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281443 1072 #uuid "babeb7e0-0816-57e1-ad72-2f46bc3bd937" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281443 1074 "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(z)}^{{v}_{4}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{3}(x,z)}^{{u}_{4}(x,z)}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,\\,(x+1)^{3}\\le z\\le 2-{x}^{2},\\,\\,x+z\\le y\\le z+2}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $y,$ the $y$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{(x+1)^{3}}^{2-{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{x+z}^{z+2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{x+z}^{z+2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(z)}^{{v}_{4}(z)}{\\int }_{x+z}^{z+2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f586edef4202e3f3de09fe947c955de62efa35e421e85b3e5034efd375790a7.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type I region in the $xz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,(x+1)^{3}\\le z\\le 2-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z,$ we split $D$ into two type II regions.\n\n![](images/ec/ec9845e347959ff1ac43a12d03261b1eb31172f14b56269d7bbfcb86a2a23e48.png)\n\nNotice that the bottom and top boundaries of the lower region, written in the form $x=x(z),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $x=-1\\,$ and $\\,x=\\sqrt[3]{z}-1.$\n\nAlso, the bottom and top boundaries of the upper region, written in the form $x=x(z),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $x=-\\sqrt{2-z}\\,$ and $\\,x=0.$\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,-1\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{z}-1}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 2,\\,-\\sqrt{2-z}\\le x\\le 0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\sqrt[3]{z}-1}{\\int }_{x+z}^{z+2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2-z}}^{0}{\\int }_{x+z}^{z+2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281444 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280971 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281444 1069 "q-162324" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281444 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281444 1071 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i+x\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281444 1072 #uuid "268c6ade-bfb9-5104-a3ea-6d4f751fe4fb" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281444 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))+0\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)) \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)-\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)-\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)}{2}+\\frac{\\pi t}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)}{4}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =-[0+\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0-(0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0)] \\\\  & =-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281445 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280899 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281445 1069 "q-162325" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281445 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281445 1071 "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{z}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}z{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.$*" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281445 1072 #uuid "0f6f33b6-ca94-5283-b578-c8f6fe4dfa5c" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281445 1074 "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}z{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,z\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{3}}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $y,$ the $y$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}z{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/54/54d8420870ea92e42811a13ed2c4563c5c9ccb674ee8c3b6a277186c0fba21da.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type II region in the $xz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,z\\le x\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region $D,$ written in the form $z=z(x),$ is\n\n$$\nx=z\\,⟹\\,z=x\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nz=0.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le x},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/7f/7f19b63139a3d38c321e9938c1e8fbbf84e924df51d9b9b057d0151fc269550d.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{z}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}z{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}z{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}[z{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}z[{e}^{y}]_{0}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}z({e}^{{x}^{3}}-1)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[({e}^{{x}^{3}}-1){\\int }_{0}^{x}z\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{{x}^{3}}-1){[\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}({e}^{{x}^{3}}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}-{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[e-1-1+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{e-2}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281446 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281288 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281446 1069 "q-162330" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281446 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281446 1071 "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡y}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.$*" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281446 1072 #uuid "55c78036-de48-5eea-9253-f540a61a0813" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281446 1074 "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\operatorname{arccos}⁡y,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/c5/c5f4ec9b9b12411f3057bc074a14a1f541c768c30d3a9746d7a0484b0491ad2e.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type I region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le \\operatorname{arccos}⁡y}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\ny=0\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region $D,$ written in the form $y=y(z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡y\\,⟹\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡z.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡z},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/07/077fc8133673cd57aba570cca9a6e0f5f25aa742720948c46db2b9e1d05add69.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡y}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}y\\,[x]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}2y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{y}^{2}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2z)\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[z+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2z]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281447 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281266 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281447 1069 "q-162331" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281447 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281447 1071 "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z)/2}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{2yz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{z})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.$*" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281447 1072 #uui