ero only at the right and left sides. \n\n![](images/86/8652a197f603c517cd462c69473e3a693ba8dcd76340b6241f890663356e00cb.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the right surface. On this surface, we have $y=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le z\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,3{x}^{2}y+2z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,6{x}^{2}+2z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}6{x}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6{x}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}6{x}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2{x}^{3}+2xz]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}16+4z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[16z+2{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the left surface. On this surface, we have $y=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le z\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,3{x}^{2}y+2z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-2z\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-2{∬}_{D}z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-2\\cdot [x]_{0}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =18+(-2) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281504 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281280 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281504 1069 "q-162563" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281504 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281504 1071 "![](images/63/638a02dec15c2cecd8563854c2fb071ff70f10004f60339ef92d0914e7198ebc.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨4{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281504 1072 #uuid "bdcab013-1b4f-5535-a84d-7952ce7d745b" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281504 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $x$-component of $F=⟨4{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $x$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the front and back sides. \n\n![](images/4f/4fd228a9bf0a2d8382139d3f804ee7c9b3fd8721c8ad079de43e61b9e8947240.png)\n\n  \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the front surface. On this surface, we have $x=1$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ and $0\\le z\\le 3.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=1$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨4{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =4+3{y}^{2}+2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}4+3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4+3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4+3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[(4+2z)y+{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}4+2z+1\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2z+5\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[{z}^{2}+5z]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =9+15 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the back surface. On this surface, we have $x=0$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ and $0\\le z\\le 3.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨4{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-(3{y}^{2}+2z).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-(3{y}^{2}+2z)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[{y}^{3}+2yz]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}1+2z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{[z+{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-(3+9) \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =24+(-12) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281188 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281505 1069 "q-162569" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281505 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281505 1071 "![](images/58/581ac462bfcfead22a6c5ad4eb247f05adb1a677e92cac51954b0746cd083ab7.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨x-y,\\,y+z,\\,2x-3z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $3$ whose base is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281505 1072 #uuid "3c0ba9cf-530e-5fff-bddf-77afd0f80ef9" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281505 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2x-3z) \\\\  & =1+1+(-3) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid cylinder enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}-1\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =-{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =-\\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =-\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-12\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281506 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280995 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281506 1069 "q-162572" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281506 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281506 1071 "![](images/aa/aac27058402f89bef73700129a7812b3cf4f971c11975fc1711a46b8d10e61fe.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨3x+y{e}^{z},\\,xy+2,\\,1-xz⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281506 1072 #uuid "c50889a1-249a-5104-a142-d8d313afba10" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281506 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x+y{e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+2)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(1-xz) \\\\  & =3+x+(-x) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the rectangular solid enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281507 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280967 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281507 1069 "q-162578" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281507 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281507 1071 "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z-4)^{2}=4$ and the vector field $F=z\\,i+6y\\,j+(x+3z)\\,k.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281507 1072 #uuid "e9b12219-fd15-591a-8410-7eb56a488ff2" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281507 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+3z) \\\\  & =0+6+3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $2$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}9\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =9{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =96\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281508 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281038 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281508 1069 "q-162580" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281508 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281508 1071 "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by $(x+2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ and the vector field $F=({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,i+4y\\,j+2xz\\,k.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281508 1072 #uuid "8a625adb-c2bb-5fe3-8ab2-cff87a075be0" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281508 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xz) \\\\  & =-2x+4+2x \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $1$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281509 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281058 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281509 1069 "q-162588" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281509 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281509 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ and the surfaces $z=y$ and $z={x}^{2}+y.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(x+y)\\,i-2y\\,j+{x}^{2}z\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281509 1072 #uuid "34056bf8-ebf0-50cf-86bc-5d67c31ea40b" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281509 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}z) \\\\  & =1+(-2)+{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}({x}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type I solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,y\\le z\\le {x}^{2}+y}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281510 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280848 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281510 1069 "q-162589" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281510 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281510 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1,$ and the surfaces $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and $y=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=({y}^{3}-z)\\,i+z\\,j+2xyz\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281510 1072 #uuid "8e6de64a-d194-59f6-b620-cf3ddbd502d1" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281510 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}-z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xyz) \\\\  & =0+0+2xy \\\\  & =2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}2xy\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type III solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2xy\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}^{2}\\,2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281511 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281143 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281511 1069 "q-162594" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281511 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281511 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=4,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={x}^{2}y\\,i+2z\\,j+xyz\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281511 1072 #uuid "7e758b54-bd1f-5329-88cb-61523affaf73" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281511 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xyz) \\\\  & =2xy+0+xy \\\\  & =3xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3xy\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}3xy\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}3\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=4}\\cdot [3z]_{z=1}^{z=2} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (8-0)\\cdot (6-3) \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281512 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280869 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281512 1069 "q-162596" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281512 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281512 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=1,$ $x=e,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=0,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,i+{z}^{2}\\,j+(x-{y}^{3})\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281512 1072 #uuid "dea24fde-a211-5010-b92d-b13afeca9f75" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281512 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x-{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}+0+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]_{x=1}^{x=e}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\cdot [z]_{z=0}^{z=2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\cdot (\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2})\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281513 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281252 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281513 1069 "q-162599" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281513 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281513 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=1,$ $y=2,$ $z=1,$ $z=3.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={x}^{2}\\,i+2y\\,j+(x+{y}^{2})\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281513 1072 #uuid "e7cc5e4b-aa70-5d55-82a4-140961e3cc36" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281513 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2x+2+0 \\\\  & =2x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}(2x+2)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(2x+2)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}+2x]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}+2(1)-0)\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281514 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280895 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281514 1069 "q-162600" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281514 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281514 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=1,$ $y=2,$ $z=-1,$ $z=1.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=2y\\,i+x{y}^{2}\\,j+2xyz\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281514 1072 #uuid "a55db798-fcb0-54c3-9d76-3d7d1e841ffa" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281514 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xyz) \\\\  & =0+2xy+2xy \\\\  & =4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4xy\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}4xy\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=1}^{y=2}\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (4-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281515 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280952 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281515 1069 "q-162620" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281515 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281515 1071 "Let $G$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}],$ where $a,b\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The zero matrix $O=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $G.$\n- For the zero matrix $O$ and any $A\\in G,$ we have $A-O=A.$\n- For any $A,B\\in G,$ we have $A-B=B-A.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281515 1072 #uuid "4ab2ad7f-5552-5a07-8645-8d079346b7d7" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281515 1074 "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,$O=[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}]$ with $a=b=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, as for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ with real entries, we have\n\n$$\nA-O=A+(-O)=A+O=A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, let's consider the matrices $O=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Then $O$ and ${I}_{2}$ belong to $G,$ and we\nhave \n\n$$\n{I}_{2}-O={I}_{2}\n$$\n\n \nbut \n\n$$\nO-{I}_{2}=-{I}_{2}\\ne {I}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281516 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280986 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281516 1069 "q-162634" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281516 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281516 1071 "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)={t}^{4}\\,i+2\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne .\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281516 1072 #uuid "a2d4d750-ad01-510f-bf93-021354a0a535" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281516 1074 "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component ${t}^{4}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $2\\operatorname{csc}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$, $t\\ne n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component ${e}^{t}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281517 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280922 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281517 1069 "q-162635" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281517 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281517 1071 "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)={e}^{1-t}\\,i+(2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t+1)\\,j+t\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne .\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281517 1072 #uuid "71e68341-1ca1-5cd9-a596-b9b9c44a446b" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281517 1074 "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component ${e}^{1-t}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t+1$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$, $t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281518 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280976 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281518 1069 "q-162636" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281518 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281518 1071 "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\,i+(t+1)^{2}\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne .\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281518 1072 #uuid "540767cb-cd6f-5ce4-bc0f-7ec2c2e165a4" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281518 1074 "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\operatorname{cot}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$, $t\\ne n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $(t+1)^{2}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281519 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281087 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281519 1069 "q-162643" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281519 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281519 1071 "![](images/ec/ec31e20140609e972eb1a5878c14d0b656dc5e931cc3deb7cc03ad2b4936bdef.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation that acts on vectors in ${R}^{2}$ as follows (in the order given):\n\n- it shears vectors by a shear factor of $2$ in the $x$-direction, and then\n\n- reflects the result in the $y$-axis.\n\nWhat is the image of the unit square shown above under the action of the linear transformation?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281519 1072 #uuid "57eb91ed-d0db-57e3-b546-766ff0c0bbda" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281519 1074 "First, we apply the shear by a shear factor of $2$ in the $x$-direction:\n\n![](images/87/87247f5632dc5afb7e57d77227553cb0efd67872cc40a0e8d2804422284f7f64.png)\n\nThen, we apply the reflection in the $y$-axis:\n\n![](images/db/db2c82f441b8edaf821175b130a08f5a5ef3f18ab37282d9bb404e39e2f567d3.png)" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281520 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280847 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281520 1069 "q-162645" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281520 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281520 1071 "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)={t}^{3}\\,i+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,j+\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne .\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281520 1072 #uuid "355588e1-74ab-5c1c-9491-56bf772d1b60" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281520 1074 "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component ${t}^{3}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ except for the values of $t$ for which\n\n$$\nt+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281521 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281130 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281521 1069 "q-162646" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281521 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281521 1071 "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+({t}^{3}+1)\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne .\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281521 1072 #uuid "64d08a7f-4970-5ccb-b63d-6959013107b7" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281521 1074 "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2t)$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ except for the values of $t$ for which\n\n$$\n2t=\\pi n\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{\\pi n}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component 