operatorname{ln}⁡(2-s)$ is $s\\in (-\\infty ,2).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\sqrt{3}$ is $s\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/80/808abeaa59d7bcea3b24e741fde5296791de32b4170d60f05f2a5af203c20ba2.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)$ is $s\\in [0,2).$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281529 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281064 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281529 1069 "q-162661" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281529 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281529 1071 "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)=⟨\\frac{1}{s-1},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡s,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s-2)⟩.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281529 1072 #uuid "7c1f4ba8-733f-52ec-ba54-2cc86581e097" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281529 1074 "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components. \n\n- The domain of $\\frac{1}{s-1}$ is $s\\in (-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡s$ is $s\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s-2)$ is  $s\\in (2,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/ac/ac5fed5129673869c7b9e0da3bd46f224aedbfda11b3a26e507b533d66c4e4e9.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)$ is $s\\in (2,\\infty ).$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281530 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281020 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281530 1069 "q-162662" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281530 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281530 1071 "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=⟨\\sqrt{t},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1),\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281530 1072 #uuid "0b92442b-8b2c-57b7-8241-452f363996f5" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281530 1074 "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components. \n\n- The domain of $\\sqrt{t}$ is $t\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)$ is $s\\in (-1,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of $\\frac{1}{t}$ is  $t\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/97/971a65619088884002784f5abb3c4f3dc9b2e17e6b905f171b8106219a139557.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is $t\\in (0,\\infty ).$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281531 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281120 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281531 1069 "q-162664" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281531 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281531 1071 "Let $Q$ be the set of all functions of the form $f(x)=a{x}^{2},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- There is a function $f(x)\\in Q,$ such that $(-1)\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n- For any $f(x)\\in Q$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).$\n- For any $f(x),g(x)\\in Q$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281531 1072 #uuid "ff69a5c1-505a-5719-b6b9-7ec69e0931ef" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281531 1074 "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider the null function $f(x)=0=0{x}^{2}\\in Q.$ Then,\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot f(x)=(-1)\\cdot 0=0=f(x).\n$$\n\nHence, $(-1)\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any function $f(x)\\in Q$ and any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any functions $f(x),g(x)\\in Q$ and any fixed $x\\in R,$ the results of $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280882 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281532 1069 "q-162665" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281532 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281532 1071 "Let $S$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients and whose degree is at most $2.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\in S$\n- For any $p(x)\\in S,$ we have $(-1)\\cdot p(x)\\in S$\n- For any $p(x),q(x)\\in S$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (p(x)-q(x))=\\alpha p(x)-\\alpha q(x)$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281532 1072 #uuid "69e25145-91f1-58d1-afd8-be54a0c5863e" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281532 1074 "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $S$ contains only polynomials whose degree is at most $2.$ However, $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ is *not* a polynomial. So, $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\notin S.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $deg(q)$ the degree of a polynomial $q.$ Then, for any $p(x)\\in S$ we have that $(-1)\\cdot p(x)$ is also a polynomial, and that\n\n$$\ndeg((-1)\\cdot p)=deg(-1)+deg(p)=0+deg(p)\\le 2.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-1)p(x)\\in S.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x),q(x)\\in S$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the values $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (p(x)-q(x))=\\alpha p(x)-\\alpha q(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281533 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281017 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281533 1069 "q-162668" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281533 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281533 1071 "Let $Y$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices with determinant $1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- For any $A\\in Y,$ we have $(-1)\\cdot A=-A.$\n- For any $A\\in Y$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.$\n- For any $A\\in Y$ we have $2A\\in Y.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281533 1072 #uuid "2124447c-ee22-58ee-9afe-87a9532907c6" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281533 1074 "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in Y,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, we have\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot {a}_{ij}=-{a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot A=-A.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in Y,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta ){a}_{ij}=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+\\beta {a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the identity matrix ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in Y,$ however $2{I}_{2}\\notin Y$ because\n\n$$\n|2{I}_{2}|=|2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]|=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|=2\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 0=4\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281534 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280941 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281534 1069 "q-162673" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281534 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281534 1071 "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{t+1} \\\\ {e}^{t} \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281534 1072 #uuid "5b637ea2-eaa0-55d8-b435-a20c614987db" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281534 1074 "To find the domain of $f,$ we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components. \n\n- The domain of $\\sqrt{t+1}$ is $t\\in [-1,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of ${e}^{t}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of $2t$ is  $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/3f/3f1ca1c3d6ffd56fe0686ee209c967775b5daf09a99e80a3f38ad0a56f4424ce.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is $t\\in [-1,\\infty ).$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281535 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281271 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281535 1069 "q-162675" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281535 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281535 1071 "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{t}{2} \\\\ {t}^{3}-t \\\\ \\sqrt{1-t}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281535 1072 #uuid "dc660d5b-b6be-5eb8-9600-3269f3390d79" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281535 1074 "To find the domain of $f,$ we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components. \n\n- The domain of $\\frac{t}{2}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of ${t}^{3}-t$ is  $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of $\\sqrt{1-t}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,1].$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/da/daf03cb538fcc1546f64896cc6489beae084880feec565229e05e3db1e92b050.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,1].$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281536 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281134 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281536 1069 "q-162685" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281536 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281536 1071 "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-4}\\,i+t\\,j+\\sqrt{t-2}\\,k.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281536 1072 #uuid "49867506-48a9-52ef-9b83-53c3f3a13af0" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281536 1074 "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-4}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty -2)\\cup (-2,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\sqrt{t-2}$ is $t\\in [2,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/18/18bd3e410f2f3e415e8164ae6e2f9e03a2f7281ce15e6e05cb570ecfdac10964.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is $t\\in (2,\\infty ).$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281537 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280917 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281537 1069 "q-162687" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281537 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281537 1071 "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=\\sqrt{2t+4}\\,i+({3}^{t}+{t}^{3})\\,j+\\frac{1}{t+2}\\,k.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281537 1072 #uuid "ffdda774-d097-5dfc-84f2-f0aed3d09ee2" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281537 1074 "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\sqrt{2t+4}$ is $t\\in [-2,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component ${3}^{t}+{t}^{3}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\frac{1}{t+2}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (-2,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/51/51c8f87355b42610cd67ce1651543c462f7ea4a980202844e9940514f431b412.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is $t\\in (-2,\\infty ).$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281538 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280923 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281538 1069 "q-162704" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281538 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281538 1071 "The matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nrepresents the linear transformation that acts on a vector in ${R}^{2}$ as follows (in the order given):\n\n- It rotates the vector  $\\pi $ radians counterclockwise about the origin, then\n\n- stretches the result by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $x$-axis, and finally\n\n- reflects the result about the $x$-axis.\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281538 1072 #uuid "9939f92b-9874-5d18-8b59-989570a881e7" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281538 1074 "Let $P$ be the matrix that represents the rotation, $Q$ be the matrix that represents the stretching, and $R$ be the matrix that represents the reflection. These are given by the following elementary transformation matrices:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nHence, the combined transformation can be represented by the matrix $RQP:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RQP & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$a+b=(-3)+1=-2.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281539 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281284 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281539 1069 "q-162746" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281539 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281539 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.06$ | $0.04$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.5$ | $0.24$ | $0.06$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n| | $x$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ | |  | | $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.6$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ |  |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.6$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=1,Y=1)=P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281539 1072 #uuid "b5dd282b-2dab-5297-89c3-a4849921b265" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281539 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=1,Y=1)=f(1,1)=0.06.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.2\\cdot 0.3=0.06.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Checking some possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =0.1\\ne 0.2\\cdot 0.6={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281540 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281026 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281540 1069 "q-162760" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281540 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281540 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2\\,$ on the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8=0.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281540 1072 #uuid "46c43068-59bd-54f5-b56f-0c41ad44d87a" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281540 1074 "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨8x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=8\\lambda x \\\\ 2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(1-4\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(1-\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-8 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-2\\sqrt{2}),(0,2\\sqrt{2}).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1-\\frac{1}{4})=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-\\sqrt{2},0),(\\sqrt{2},0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0),\\,(0,\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ | $(0,\\pm 2\\sqrt{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $4$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ and its minimum value is $4.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280949 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281541 1069 "q-162763" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281541 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281541 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=4y-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281541 1072 #uuid "932d87ff-4a97-5b0b-b8d7-da2ba43e7f5d" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281541 1074 "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=5\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-4x,4-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 4-2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(\\lambda +2)=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1)=2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, 