system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- First, assume that $\\lambda \\ne \\frac{1}{9},$ $\\lambda \\ne \\frac{1}{4}$ and $\\lambda \\ne 1.$ Then, the solution of the above system is $x=y=z=0.$ But this contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=36.$\n\n- Next, assume that $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{9}.$ Then, by the second and third equations, we must have $y=z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}=36\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 2,0,0).$\n\n- Now, assume that $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}.$ Then, by the first and third equations, we must have $x=z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n4{y}^{2}=36\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 3.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 3,0).$\n\n- Finally, assume that $\\lambda =1.$ Then, by the first and second equations, we must have $x=y=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}=36\\,⇒\\,z=\\pm 6.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0,\\pm 6).$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(\\pm 2,0,0)\\,$ | $\\,(0,\\pm 3,0)\\,$ | $\\,(0,0,\\pm 6)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ | $\\,9\\,$ | $\\,36\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(\\pm 2,0,0),$ and its minimum value is $4.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281549 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281181 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281549 1069 "q-162780" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281549 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281549 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-8{y}^{2}$ given that ${x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}\\le 25.$ \n\n*Hint: The critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region ${x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}<25$ is $(0,0).$*" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281549 1072 #uuid "e45729b0-5cc3-5497-8a8a-a3af06f9f95a" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281549 1074 "We are given that the critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region's interior is $(0,0).$\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}=25.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=25\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-16y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,10y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ -16y=10\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}2x(1-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ -2y(8+5\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=25$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2} & =25\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}).$\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=25$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =25\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(\\pm 5,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}),\\,(5,0),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})$ | $(\\pm 5,0)$ | $(0,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $-40$ | $25$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}),$ and its minimum value is $-40.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281139 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281550 1069 "q-162782" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281550 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281550 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-2{x}^{2}$ given that $2{x}^{2}+y\\le 1.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281550 1072 #uuid "6c8aaf19-7032-5c17-8488-1228e55d6f6b" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281550 1074 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-4x,2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+y=1.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-4x,2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =1$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 1,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4x=4\\lambda x \\\\ 2y=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(1+\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ y=\\frac{\\lambda }{2}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,1).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation gives $y=-\\frac{1}{2},$ and the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(0,1),\\,(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,1)$ | $(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}),$ and its minimum value is $-\\frac{5}{4}.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281551 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281272 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281551 1069 "q-162784" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281551 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281551 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+3$ on the surface ${z}^{2}-xy-4=0.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global minimum of $f(x,y,z)$ exists on the surface.*" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281551 1072 #uuid "e0089be7-076e-5546-9485-8927c1e7080e" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281551 1074 "We need to find the minimum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={z}^{2}-xy-4=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨-y,-x,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x=-\\lambda y \\\\ 2y=-\\lambda x \\\\ 2z=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}(4-{\\lambda }^{2})x=0 \\\\ (4-{\\lambda }^{2})y=0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- First, assume that $\\lambda \\ne 1$ and $\\lambda \\ne \\pm 2.$ Then, the solution of the above system is $x=y=z=0.$ But this contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0.$\n\n- Next, assume that $\\lambda =-2.$ Then we must have $x=y$ and $z=0.$ But this also contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0.$\n\n- Now, assume that $\\lambda =1.$ Then, by the first and second equations, we must have $x=y=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}-4=0\\,⇒\\,z=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0,\\pm 2).$\n\n- Finally, assume that $\\lambda =2.$ Then we must have $x=-y$ and $z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n-(-y)y-4=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(∓2,\\pm 2,0).$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(0,0,\\pm 2)\\,$ | $\\,(∓2,\\pm 2,0)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ | $\\,11\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(0,0,\\pm 2),$ and its minimum value is $7.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281552 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281283 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281552 1069 "q-162786" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281552 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281552 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=-2x+y-2\\,$ on the parabola ${x}^{2}-y=1.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global minimum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the parabola.*" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281552 1072 #uuid "21099a0a-2aea-5107-84bf-b84b153e7a84" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281552 1074 "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}-y=1.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2=2\\lambda x \\\\ 1=-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ \\lambda =-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use $\\lambda $ to find $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ into the condition $g(x,y)=0$ and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-y & =1 \\\\ 1-y & =1 \\\\ y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has a possible extreme value at the point\n\n$$\n(1,0).\n$$\n\nSince the given parabola opens upwards, there isn't a maximum value of $f$ on the parabola. Thus, the extreme value is the minimum of $f$ on the parabola. We evaluate $f$ at this point:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(1,0)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(1,0),$ and its minimum value is $-4.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281553 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281061 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281553 1069 "q-162788" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281553 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281553 1071 "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, find the minimum possible sum of three positive numbers whose product is $27.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281553 1072 #uuid "b8c68071-089c-5bff-bc03-ba61a8eab03b" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281553 1074 "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the three numbers. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x+y+z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\nxyz=27.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=27\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1=\\lambda yz \\\\ 1=\\lambda xz \\\\ 1=\\lambda xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this would give $1=0.$ Therefore, from the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}yz & =xz \\\\ yz-xz & =0 \\\\ (y-x)z & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z>0,$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y=x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xyz & =27 \\\\ x(x)(x) & =27 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =27 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(3,3,3),$ and the minimum value is \n\n$$\nf(3,3,3)=3+3+3=9.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the minimum (and not the maximum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(9,3,1) & =9+3+1 \\\\  & =13 \\\\  & >f(3,3,3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281554 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281166 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281554 1069 "q-162790" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281554 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281554 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=x-{y}^{2}$ given that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281554 1072 #uuid "b349e078-f370-57a4-8287-923743beec39" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281554 1074 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}1=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda x=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y(1+\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 2,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-1$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system gives \n\n$$\n-x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm 2,0),\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{17}{4}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(2,0),$ and its maximum value is $2.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281555 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281237 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281555 1069 "q-162791" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281555 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281555 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=-4x+y+4\\,$ on the parabola $8x-{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global maximum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the parabola.*" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281555 1072 #uuid "7651aa63-b4a3-56d9-b622-a30f9579f57e" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281555 1074 "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=8x-{y}^{2}=1.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-4,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨8,-2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4=8\\lambda  \\\\ 1=-2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use $\\lambda $ to find $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2(-\\frac{1}{2})}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expression for $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=1$ and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-{y}^{2}=1 \\\\ 8x-1 & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has a possible extreme value at the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{4},1).\n$$\n\nSince the given parabola opens to the right, there isn't a minimum value of $f$ on the parabola. Thus, the extreme value is the maximum of $f$ on the parabola. We evaluate $f$ at this point:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{1}{4},1)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{1}{4},1),$ and its maximum value is $4.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281556 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280845 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281556 1069 "q-162799" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281556 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281556 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4$ given that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x-2y\\le 6.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281556 1072 #uuid "a9f72078-f37a-504f-b398-fa5acaea8b10" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281556 1074 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x-2y=6.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=6\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x-2,2y-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =1$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 1,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda (x-1) \\\\ 2y=2\\lambda (y-1)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{\\lambda }{\\lambda -1} \\\\ y=\\frac{\\lambda }{\\lambda -1}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,x=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ in the condition $g(x,y)=6,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x-2y & =6 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2}-2x-2x & =6 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x & =6 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x-6 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x-3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(-1,-1),\\,(3,3).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(-1,-1)$ | $(3,3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $4$ | $6$ | $22$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,0),$ and its minimum value is $4.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281557 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280999 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281557 1069 "q-162801" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281557 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281557 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ given that $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281557 1072 #uuid "172e1cdb-aee2-5490-b82d-56a90e36d5c9" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281557 1074 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨8x,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $x=y=0$ which yields the critical point $(0,0).$ Notice that this point is not in the region's interior. However, it is in the boundary of the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨8x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2(x-1),2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}8x=2\\lambda (x-1) \\\\ -2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}4x=\\lambda (x-1) \\\\ y(\\lambda +1)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{0}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,x=1\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(2,0)$ and $(0,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-1$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system implies \n\n$$\n4x=-(x-1)\\,⇒\\,x=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{5}-1)}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\frac{1}{5},\\pm \\frac{3}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(\\frac{1}{5},\\pm \\frac{3}{5}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(\\frac{1}{5},\\pm \\frac{3}{5})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $16$ | $-\\frac{1}{5}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(2,0),$ and its maximum value is $16.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281558 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281050 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281558 1069 "q-162802" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281558 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281558 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ given that $4{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 1.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281558 1072 #uuid "46d9a0e8-afba-50ff-93a4-8a5c09128d7d" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281558 1074 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $x=y=0$ which yields the critical point $(0,0).$ Notice that this point is not in the region's interior. However, it is in the boundary of the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1.$\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨8x,2(y-1)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=8\\lambda x \\\\ -2y=2\\lambda (y-1)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(1-4\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y=-\\lambda (y-1)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,y=1\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0)$ and $(0,2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{4}(y-1)\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{5}-1)}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\frac{3}{10},\\frac{1}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(\\pm \\frac{3}{10},\\frac{1}{5})\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(\\pm \\frac{3}{10},\\frac{1}{5})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-4$ | $\\frac{1}{20}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,2),$ and its minimum value is $-4.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281559 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281225 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281559 1069 "q-162824" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281559 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281559 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.35$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n| | $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.5$ | $0.5$ | |  | | $y$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.6$ | $0.4$ | |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.5$ | $0.5$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.6$ | $0.4$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=1,Y=0)=P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=0)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281559 1072 #uuid "745e2846-c808-51fa-a431-23f636897e08" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281559 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=1,Y=0)=f(1,0)=0.25.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=0)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0)=0.5\\cdot 0.6=0.3.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(X=1,Y=0)\\ne P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=0),\n$$\n\nand $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281560 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281257 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281560 1069 "q-162886" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281560 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281560 1071 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=({t}^{2}+t)\\,i+({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,j+(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,0).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i+z\\,j+x\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281560 1072 #uuid "915ccbd6-d5b8-5b79-ba41-8aee577baac0" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281560 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+t,\\,y={t}^{3}+{t}^{2},\\,z=2{t}^{2}+2t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+z\\,j+x\\,k \\\\  & =({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,i+(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\,j+({t}^{2}+t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\,k \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\,j+(4t+2)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,i+(2{t}^{2}+2t)\\,j+({t}^{2}+t)\\,k)\\cdot ((2t+1)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\,j+(4t+2)\\,k) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})(2t+1)+(2{t}^{2}+2t)(3{t}^{2}+2t)+({t}^{2}+t)(4t+2) \\\\  & =2t+11{t}^{2}+17{t}^{3}+8{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(2t+11{t}^{2}+17{t}^{3}+8{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[{t}^{2}+\\frac{11}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{17}{4}{t}^{4}+\\frac{8}{5}{t}^{5}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(1-\\frac{11}{3}+\\frac{17}{4}-\\frac{8}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281561 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281110 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281561 1069 "q-162887" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281561 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281561 1071 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=({t}^{3}-t)\\,i+(2{t}^{2}+1)\\,j+({t}^{2}-1)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i+3\\,j+yz\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281561 1072 #uuid "c852df75-6989-5a7d-bc7f-0618226da77b" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281561 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-t,\\,y=2{t}^{2}+1,\\,z={t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+3\\,j+yz\\,k \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}+1)\\,i+3\\,j+(2{t}^{2}+1)({t}^{2}-1)\\,k \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}+1)\\,i+3\\,j+(2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}-1)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\,k \\\\  & =(3{t}^{2}-1)\\,i+4t\\,j+2t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((2{t}^{2}+1)\\,i+3\\,j+(2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}-1)\\,k)\\cdot ((3{t}^{2}-1)\\,i+4t\\,j+2t\\,k) \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}+1)(3{t}^{2}-1)+3(4t)+(2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}-1)(2t) \\\\  & =-1+10t+{t}^{2}-2{t}^{3}+6{t}^{4}+4{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-1+10t+{t}^{2}-2{t}^{3}+6{t}^{4}+4{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-t+5{t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{4}+\\frac{6}{5}{t}^{5}+\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{6}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-1+5+\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{2}{3})-(1+5-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{12}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281562 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281112 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281562 1069 "q-162892" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281562 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281562 1071 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=2y\\,i-2x\\,j+{e}^{z-2y}\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281562 1072 #uuid "203be68d-096d-564c-bf44-7d62e44d7418" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281562 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2y\\,i-2x\\,j+{e}^{z-2y}\\,k \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)}\\,k \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+k)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+1\\cdot 2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =-2\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)-2\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =-2\\pi +2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-2\\pi +2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[-2\\pi t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(-2\\pi (1)+0)-(-2\\pi (-1)+0) \\\\  & =-4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [1759218628156