1)?$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281564 1072 #uuid "d8e27fa4-3108-5d1b-bf36-7c34fcdd2cd8" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281564 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, since our random variables are independent, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,-1) & ={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(-1) \\\\ P(X=2,Y=-1) & =P(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=-1) \\\\ 0.03 & =0.1\\cdot P(Y=-1) \\\\ P(Y=-1) & =\\frac{0.03}{0.1} \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281565 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280881 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281565 1069 "q-162974" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281565 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281565 1071 "The lamina $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1}$ has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{y}.$ Find the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281565 1072 #uuid "048f739d-8a1c-5493-b18b-4a60653b31c4" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281565 1074 "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is represented as a type I plane region and $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{y}.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot \\frac{15{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}15{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}15{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}15{x}^{2}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}15{x}^{2}\\cdot [y]_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}15{x}^{2}\\cdot (1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}15{x}^{2}-15{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[5{x}^{3}-3{x}^{5}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281566 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281232 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281566 1069 "q-162977" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281566 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281566 1071 "The lamina $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 3,\\,1\\le x\\le {e}^{y}}$ has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{xy}.$ Find the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281566 1072 #uuid "9b4076e3-ab6e-5bdd-85c0-2781a4c652d0" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281566 1074 "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is represented as a type II plane region and $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{xy}.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot \\frac{1}{xy}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{y}}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]_{x=1}^{x={e}^{y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=1}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(9-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281567 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280970 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281567 1069 "q-162981" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281567 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281567 1071 "Consider a thin rod along the $x$-axis over the interval $0\\le x\\le 4$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)=\\sqrt{x}.$ Find the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass of the rod." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281567 1072 #uuid "24e60831-e83f-5ffa-a929-6a3941104ab1" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281567 1074 "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=\\sqrt{x},\\,a=0,\\,b=4.\n$$\n\nSo, the mass of the rod is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3/2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}x\\cdot \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5/2}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {4}^{5/2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{64}{5})}{(\\frac{16}{3})}=\\frac{12}{5}.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281568 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280854 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281568 1069 "q-162984" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281568 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281568 1071 "A thin plate $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{3}{y}$ is enclosed between the curve $y=2-{x}^{2}$ and  the line $y=1$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1.$ Find the moment of the plate about the $x$-axis." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281568 1072 #uuid "bdacf202-231e-52d3-bf9c-5334e7620bfe" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281568 1074 "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is a type I plane region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/2d/2d08bcbbcd7ec4d8c388d24728c3f3afdc7c954497f342e9ac2b459fad24e5d2.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2-{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{3}{y}.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot \\frac{3}{y}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2-{x}^{2}}3\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}3[y]_{y=1}^{y=2-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}3[2-{x}^{2}-1]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}3-3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[3x-{x}^{3}]}_{x=-1}^{x=1} \\\\  & =(3-1)-(-3+1) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281569 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281150 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281569 1069 "q-163013" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281569 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281569 1071 "The mass density function $\\lambda ,$ measured in $\\text{kg/m},$ of a thin rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $1\\le x\\le 2$ is given by $\\lambda (x)=3{x}^{2}+1,$ where $x$ is measured in meters. What is the total mass of the rod?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281569 1072 #uuid "2a1a732b-ce54-5948-8e46-431b119e1f0d" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281569 1074 "Given a thin rod oriented along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b,$ if $\\lambda (x)$ denotes the mass density function of the rod, then the total mass of the rod is given by  \n\n$$\nm={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=3{x}^{2}+1,\\,a=1,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe calculate the mass of the rod as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}3{x}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3}+x]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =({2}^{3}+2)-({1}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the rod is $8\\,\\text{kg}.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281570 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281084 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281570 1069 "q-163016" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281570 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281570 1071 "Consider a thin rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $1\\le x\\le e$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Given that the rod's mass is $1$ unit, what is the $x$-coordinate of its center of mass?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281570 1072 #uuid "0be2d757-7e09-53f5-811d-364cfe046cee" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281570 1074 "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,a=1,\\,b=e.\n$$\n\nSo, the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[x]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{e-1}{1}=e-1.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281571 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280955 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281571 1069 "q-163017" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281571 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281571 1071 "Consider a thin rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $0\\le x\\le 1$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)=x-{x}^{3}.$ Given that the rod's mass is $\\frac{1}{4}$ units, what is the $x$-coordinate of its center of mass?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281571 1072 #uuid "b911aeca-0e09-5dc0-b2cb-6f0f1115cce0" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281571 1074 "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=x-{x}^{3},\\,a=0,\\,b=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot (x-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}-{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{2}{15})}{(\\frac{1}{4})}=\\frac{8}{15}.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281572 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281042 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281572 1069 "q-163019" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281572 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281572 1071 "![](images/88/887bc91e2565fa24630af4cb74b28723783e31f4769495f0a28a622d73d176de.png)\n\nThe thin rod above has the mass density function $\\lambda (x)=x+{x}^{2}.$ Given that the mass of the rod is $\\frac{5}{6}$ units, what is the $x$-coordinate of its center of mass?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281572 1072 #uuid "48efa884-8150-5e6b-8d0a-1ebe0194039d" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281572 1074 "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=x+{x}^{2},\\,a=0,\\,b=1.\n$$\n\nSo,  the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot (x+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{7}{12})}{(\\frac{5}{6})}=\\frac{7}{10}.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280957 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281573 1069 "q-163022" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281573 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281573 1071 "![](images/54/54e5dd5fdea01c02b968a6040068e190676841cb8d7b7fc05c561e02d522bbf1.png)\n\nThe thin rod above has the mass density function $\\lambda (x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}.$ Given that the mass of the rod is $\\frac{15}{32}$ units, what is the $x$-coordinate of its center of mass?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281573 1072 #uuid "085351ec-f493-50f6-a73c-ef9589568633" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281573 1074 "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}},\\,a=1,\\,b=4.\n$$\n\nSo,  the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{3}{4})}{(\\frac{15}{32})}=\\frac{8}{5}.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281574 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281155 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281574 1069 "q-163023" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281574 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281574 1071 "![](images/d0/d0e2bd46bf7db6e20f321383e8e0440a2a86f263ce5eb256d20db71f5c632e27.png)\n\nThe thin rod above has the mass density function $\\lambda (x)=6{x}^{2}-10\\,\\text{kg/m},$ where $x$ is measured in meters. What is the total mass of the rod?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281574 1072 #uuid "60178f8c-f591-5357-a77e-2e9d68c4a33e" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281574 1074 "Given a thin rod oriented along the  $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b,$ if $\\lambda (x)$ denotes the mass density function of the rod, then the total mass of the rod is given by      \n\n$$\nm={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=6{x}^{2}-10,\\,a=2,\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the mass of the rod as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}6{x}^{2}-10\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[2{x}^{3}-10x]_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 27-10\\cdot 3)-(2\\cdot 8-10\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the rod is $28\\,\\text{kg}.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281575 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281253 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281575 1069 "q-163024" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281575 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281575 1071 "![](images/d6/d64311921135cf8dea0ec70bb0a83909fb1dba52664f4d6080de596be1693d5e.png)\n\nThe thin rod above has the mass density function $\\lambda (x)=\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{kg/m},$ where $x$ is measured in meters. What is the total mass of the rod?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281575 1072 #uuid "b02297fd-4e01-5d21-a6d0-693013c589ed" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281575 1074 "Given a thin rod oriented along the  $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b,$ if $\\lambda (x)$ denotes the mass density function of the rod, then the total mass of the rod is given by      \n\n$$\nm={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}},\\,a=1,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the mass of the rod as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}8{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-4{x}^{-2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{4}{1}) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the rod is $3\\,\\text{kg}.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281170 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281576 1069 "q-163026" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281576 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281576 1071 "Consider a thin rod along the $x$-axis over the interval $0\\le x\\le 2$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)=8-{x}^{3}.$ Find the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass of the rod." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281576 1072 #uuid "90019212-5ef4-5914-92e3-b64c1a7668c7" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281576 1074 "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=8-{x}^{3},\\,a=0,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the mass of the rod is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}8-{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[8x-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 2-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot (8-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}8x-{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[4{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 4-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 32 \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{48}{5})}{12}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281577 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281008 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281577 1069 "q-163027" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281577 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281577 1071 "![](images/32/32500120ed36acde90f5b27c44a6cf45f2d4766bacb5eb097debc0cd7117f27f.png)\n\nThe thin rod above has the mass density function $\\lambda (x)=3{x}^{2}-x\\,\\text{kg/m},$ where $x$ is measured in meters. What is the total mass of the rod?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281577 1072 #uuid "32fa56f7-ac3c-5e91-abfd-f4c07339b203" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281577 1074 "Given a thin rod oriented along the  $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b,$ if $\\lambda (x)$ denotes the mass density function of the rod, then the total mass of the rod is given by      \n\n$$\nm={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=3{x}^{2}-x,\\,a=1,\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the mass of the rod as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}3{x}^{2}-x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(27-\\frac{9}{2})-(1-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the rod is $22\\,\\text{kg}.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281219 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281578 1069 "q-16303" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281578 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281578 1071 "![](images/6f/6f4759c4401158802661e282a44a0a84e2207ae71d28f38295fe8c6d132d7d46.png)\n\nWhat complex number is represented in the Argand diagram above?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281578 1072 #uuid "11dbe39c-5430-5835-9ad5-096ac9158a5f" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281578 1074 "The coordinates of the point are $(-2,-4).$\n\n- The real part of this complex number is $x=-2,$ and\n\n- the imaginary part is $y=-4.$\n\nTherefore, the number depicted is $z=-2-4\\text{i}.$ Finally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-2-4\\text{i} \\\\  & =-4\\text{i}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281579 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281068 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281579 1069 "q-163030" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281579 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281579 1071 "![](images/ca/ca0ff396606039accf8c3acb4fd1b8e505dd16d2fe9b765fcd5c043d1538a573.png)\n\nThe thin rod above has the mass density function $\\lambda (x)=2{x}^{3}\\,\\text{kg/m},$ where $x$ is measured in meters. What is the total mass of the rod?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281579 1072 #uuid "109a4584-2208-5339-8501-61e59c46616d" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281579 1074 "Given a thin rod oriented along the  $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b,$ if $\\lambda (x)$ denotes the mass density function of the rod, then the total mass of the rod is given by      \n\n$$\nm={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=2{x}^{3},\\,a=0,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the mass of the rod as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{4}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the rod is $8\\,\\text{kg}.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281580 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280972 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281580 1069 "q-163036" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281580 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281580 1071 "A thin rod with mass density function $\\lambda (x)=6x,$ measured in $\\text{kg/m},$ is placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $1\\le x\\le 2,$ where $x$ is measured in meters. Find the moment of the rod about the origin." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281580 1072 #uuid "9453a390-8311-5746-91ff-8745872f7e8d" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281580 1074 "The moment about the origin $O$ of a rod with mass density function $\\lambda (x)$ for $a\\le x\\le b$ is given by \n\n$$\n{M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=6x,\\,a=1,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the moment of the rod about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\cdot 6x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}6{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({2}^{3}-{1}^{3}) \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required moment is $14\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281171 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281581 1069 "q-163037" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281581 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281581 1071 "A thin rod with mass density function $\\lambda (x)=\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}},$ measured in $\\text{kg/m},$ is placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $1\\le x\\le 4,$ where $x$ is measured in meters. Find the moment of the rod about the origin." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281581 1072 #uuid "7c12a191-792b-5050-84d6-8d815e68ff76" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281581 1074 "The moment about the origin $O$ of a rod with mass density function $\\lambda (x)$ for $a\\le x\\le b$ is given by \n\n$$\n{M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}},\\,a=1,\\,b=4.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the moment of the rod about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}x\\cdot \\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{4}{x}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{4}{1}) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required moment is $3\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280937 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281582 1069 "q-163039" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281582 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281582 1071 "A thin rod with mass density function $\\lambda (x)=3x+1,$ measured in $\\text{kg/m},$ is placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $1\\le x\\le 2,$ where $x$ is measured in meters. Find the moment of the rod about the origin." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281582 1072 #uuid "5f548775-b0fd-5f5a-8099-718c65af1a69" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281582 1074 "The moment about the origin $O$ of a rod with mass density function $\\lambda (x)$ for $a\\le x\\le b$ is given by \n\n$$\n{M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=3x+1,\\,a=1,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the moment of the rod about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\cdot (3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}3{x}^{2}+x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(8+\\frac{4}{2})-(1+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{2} \\\\  & =8.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required moment is $8.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281583 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280991 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281583 1069 "q-163041" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281583 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281583 1071 "![](images/7f/7f88ca365b75320795e9d38170a8586399d759696638a4bd78c73ca6c6f44b1a.png)\n\nThe thin rod above has the mass density function $\\lambda (x)=4-x\\,\\text{kg/m}.$ Find the moment of the rod about the origin." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281583 1072 #uuid "0b7e3197-bf33-52b1-86e4-d8f2b5f29aa8" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281583 1074 "The moment about the origin $O$ of a rod with mass density function $\\lambda (x)$ for $a\\le x\\le b$ is given by \n\n$$\n{M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=4-x,\\,a=0,\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the moment of the rod about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\cdot (4-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}4x-{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot {3}^{2}-\\frac{{3}^{3}}{3})-0 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required moment is $9\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281584 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281118 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281584 1069 "q-163042" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281584 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281584 1071 "![](images/88/887bc91e2565fa24630af4cb74b28723783e31f4769495f0a28a622d73d176de.png)\n\nThe thin rod above has the mass density function $\\lambda (x)=6x+{x}^{2}\\,\\text{kg/m}.$ Find the moment of the rod about the origin." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281584 1072 #uuid "edceef54-191f-5d57-a61a-4e3776dcf8e8" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281584 1074 "The moment about the origin $O$ of a rod with mass density function $\\lambda (x)$ for $a\\le x\\le b$ is given by \n\n$$\n{M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=6x+{x}^{2},\\,a=0,\\,b=1.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the moment of the rod about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot (6x+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{3}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =2.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required moment is $2.25\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281585 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281056 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281585 1069 "q-163044" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281585 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281585 1071 "For the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}+{y}^{3},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\ncalculate ${f}^{′}$ at $a=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281585 1072 #uuid "33fd3d9e-a092-5027-a814-901fe8090b35" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281585 1074 "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{3})=2x, & \\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{3})=3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand ${f}^{′}$ at $a=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)=[\\begin{aligned}2(2) & 3(1)^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281586 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281174 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281586 1069 "q-163046" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281586 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281586 1071 "For the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x}{y},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\ncalculate ${f}^{′}$ at $a=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281586 1072 #uuid "6b4008ba-06f1-5e76-bd20-b28bf061598c" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281586 1074 "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y})=\\frac{1}{y}, & \\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y})=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand ${f}^{′}$ at $a=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1)} & -\\frac{(-2)}{(1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281587 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281255 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281587 1069 "q-163047" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281587 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281587 1071 "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}{y}^{3},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\ncalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281587 1072 #uuid "b80ece25-e7d4-5756-bce7-b1813e029457" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281587 1074 "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3})=2x{y}^{3}, & \\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{3})=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{3} & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281588 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280890 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281588 1069 "q-163053" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281588 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281588 1071 "A thin plate $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=15{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}$ is enclosed between the curve $y={x}^{2}$ and the line $y=1$ for $0\\le x\\le 1.$ Find the moment of the plate about the $x$-axis." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281588 1072 #uuid "1482f171-c56c-5bf1-b60d-7a3b492a5920" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281588 1074 "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is a type I plane region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/28/28a3f0e1eda23169a649276a9cde34d3d96cdd75d5b2bc71c3535604612526e5.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=15{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot 15{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}15{x}^{2}{y}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}15{x}^{2}{y}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}15{x}^{2}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}{y}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}15{x}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{2}{5}{y}^{5/2}]}_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{x}^{2}(1-|x{|}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{x}^{2}(1-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{x}^{2}-6{x}^{7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{3}-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{8}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =(2-\\frac{3}{4})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281589 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280989 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281589 1069 "q-163055" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281589 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281589 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-6$ given that $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}\\le 5.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281589 1072 #uuid "78ee316a-eb04-5e68-af98-0ccef9f21d29" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281589 1074 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-x,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}=5.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=5\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨\\frac{x}{2},-2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-x=\\frac{\\lambda x}{2} \\\\ 2y=-2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(\\lambda +2)=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- If $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, then assuming the constraint condition $g(x,y)=5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =5\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\sqrt{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\sqrt{20},0).$\n\n- If $\\lambda =-1$ in the second equation of the system, then from the first equation we have $x=0$, and assuming the constraint condition $g(x,y)=5$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2} & =5\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not possible.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(\\pm \\sqrt{20},0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(\\pm \\sqrt{20},0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-6$ | $-16$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(0,0),$ and its maximum value is $-6.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281590 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281045 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281590 1069 "q-163056" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281590 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281590 1071 "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\pi \\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-∗=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\pi \\end{aligned}])\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281590 1072 #uuid "723c2fc9-85ff-54eb-a36d-e043eb14286d" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281590 1074 "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy),\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\pi \\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=0,\\,{f}^{′}(a)=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi  & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-f(a)={f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nz-0=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi  & 0\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\pi \\end{aligned}]).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n0+\\pi +0=\\pi .\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281591 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281165 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281591 1069 "q-163058" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281591 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281591 1071 "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ e\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-∗=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ e\\end{aligned}])\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281591 1072 #uuid "0b9a1de6-0ca7-5f4f-b40d-58cbe6ce207e" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281591 1074 "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & \\frac{{x}^{3}}{y}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ e\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=1,\\,{f}^{′}(a)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & \\frac{1}{e}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-f(a)={f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nz-1=[\\begin{aligned}3 & \\frac{1}{e}\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ e\\end{aligned}]).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n1+3+\\frac{1}{e}=\\frac{4e+1}{e}.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281592 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280901 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281592 1069 "q-163062" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281592 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281592 1071 "The lamina $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\le y\\le 0}$ has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=2{e}^{y}.$ Find the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis." 13194139533405 1759218