5 17592186045585 true] [17592186281610 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281610 1071 "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y+1),$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=ax-y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+bxy+c{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281610 1072 #uuid "46cd9f15-1dee-5aee-b3d9-70903f558ae8" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281610 1074 "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{x-y+1}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{x-y+1} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x-y+1)^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{(x-y+1)^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x-y+1)^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈0+1\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(-1\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(1)\\cdot xy+(-1)\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1x-y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+1xy+(-\\frac{1}{2}){y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1+(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281611 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281034 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281611 1069 "q-163112" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281611 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281611 1071 "For the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}xy+{y}^{2} \\\\ xy \\\\ y+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe total derivative ${f}^{′}$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}y &  \\\\ y &  \\\\  & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom, what are the missing entries?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281611 1072 #uuid "cebdbece-6e08-53c7-9ec6-06388e9b98c6" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281611 1074 "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+{y}^{2})=y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+{y}^{2})=x+2y, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)=y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)=x, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}y & x+2y \\\\ y & x \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThus, the missing entries are $x+2y,\\,$ $x,\\,$ and $\\frac{1}{x}.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281612 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280979 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281612 1069 "q-163118" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281612 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281612 1071 "![](images/33/33d603d41e211e402165a33c98eb01965b3f49358999d0223ea320c6197525a0.png)\n\nFind the volume of the solid region above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281612 1072 #uuid "1678b5cd-d2b9-5ca9-bd33-4fcc6526c7d5" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281612 1074 "![](images/2a/2a71dcc574b324a0904b21edf1559f80d1ffe0e35ab0b0fd5cec8b31317ec548.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le y+2z},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/c0/c0bb526b2e56649ebadf7a3989340858451356e24fa205a0f3b26579f22315a2.png)\n\nHence, the type II representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le y+2z}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y+2z}dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[x]_{x=0}^{x=y+2z}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(y+2z)-0\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y+2z\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[yz+{z}^{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(y(2)+{2}^{2})-0\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2y+4\\,dy \\\\  & =[{y}^{2}+4y]_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =({2}^{2}+4(2))-0 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281613 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280867 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281613 1069 "q-163122" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281613 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281613 1071 "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the three missing expressions." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281613 1072 #uuid "e7670cca-4082-532e-925a-af41e094c0c3" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281613 1074 "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\