f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2) & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3x-y} & -{e}^{3x-y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{′}(1,2) & =[\\begin{aligned}3e & -e\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x-y} & -3{e}^{3x-y} \\\\ -3{e}^{3x-y} & {e}^{3x-y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,2) & =[\\begin{aligned}9e & -3e \\\\ -3e & e\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈e+[\\begin{aligned}3e & -e\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}9e & -3e \\\\ -3e & e\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\ne+3e+(y-2)+(y-2)+9e+(-3e)=2y+10e-4.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281617 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281105 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281617 1069 "q-163146" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281617 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281617 1071 "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3}$\nwith mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=(x+y)\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},$ where $x$ and $y$ area measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of this lamina about the $y$-axis." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281617 1072 #uuid "e73bf43d-4842-564c-b994-8b4ed88b9ee2" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281617 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}y]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}4+\\frac{8y}{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[4y+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =12+12 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $y$-axis is $24\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281618 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280975 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281618 1069 "q-163148" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281618 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281618 1071 "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=6{x}^{2}-2y+2,\\,D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $b$ given that $a=\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281618 1072 #uuid "51a515de-4536-5de9-a9e1-b88421eacc3f" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281618 1074 "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(6{x}^{2}-2y+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6{x}^{2}-2y+2)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[6{x}^{2}y-{y}^{2}+2y]_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(6{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[2{x}^{3}+x]_{x=-1}^{x=1} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot {1}^{3}+1)-(2\\cdot (-1)^{3}+(-1)) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=1\\cdot 2=2.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $a=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =f(\\frac{1}{2},b)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 6 & =(6\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2b+2)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{7}{2}-2b \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & =-2b \\\\ b & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $b=\\frac{1}{4}.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281619 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280870 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281619 1069 "q-163152" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281619 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281619 1071 "The lamina $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\le y\\le 1}$ has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Find the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281619 1072 #uuid "13f35429-2bb3-5bdd-9f21-f76be76ab433" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281619 1074 "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is represented as a type II plane region and $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}[x]_{x={y}^{2}}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}1-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=1/2}^{y=1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{3})-(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{24}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281620 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281239 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281620 1069 "q-163153" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281620 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281620 1071 "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x+4y,\\,D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2x}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=1.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281620 1072 #uuid "2753d2c4-41b4-5b63-bb59-69b4f87c935b" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281620 1074 "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(2x+4y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}(2x+4y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[2xy+2{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}12{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[4{x}^{3}]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[y]_{y=0}^{y=2x}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=1.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}32 & =f(a,1)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 32 & =(2a+4\\cdot 1)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 8 & =2a+4 \\\\ 4 & =2a \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(2,1)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=2.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281621 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280842 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281621 1069 "q-163154" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281621 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281621 1071 "A thin plate $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=x$ is enclosed between the line $y=4x$ and the $x$-axis for $0\\le x\\le 1.$ Find the moment of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281621 1072 #uuid "de505f18-8829-5988-b817-7f8f1583d7df" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281621 1074 "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is a type I plane region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/07/07701125c6acc36d127556bf95940bf0f74ba9ef3072f3af22dac26edc053a74.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 4x}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=x.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4x}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot [y]_{y=0}^{y=4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot 4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281622 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280930 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281622 1069 "q-163155" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281622 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281622 1071 "A thin plate $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=2y$ is enclosed between the line $y=\\sqrt{x}$ and the $x$-axis for $0\\le x\\le 1.$ Find the moment of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281622 1072 #uuid "d6cb70e6-635f-52ae-9613-df9cd3db0061" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281622 1074 "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is a type I plane region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/dd/ddc11fc93f034c3d650854e21f178716c941c18f12eb94123e1af2c835c47981.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=2y.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot 2y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}2y\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(x-0)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281623 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281101 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281623 1069 "q-163158" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281623 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281623 1071 "Consider the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{1}^{2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{2}^{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf the linear approximation of $f$ at $a=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\ \\sqrt{\\pi }\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}∗ & 0 \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}](x-[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\ \\sqrt{\\pi }\\end{aligned}]),\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281623 1072 #uuid "4537e764-1c1e-55e1-b386-3aa2a68f9d6e" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281623 1074 "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{1}^{2}))=2{x}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{1}^{2}),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{1}^{2}))=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{2}^{2}))=0,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{2}^{2}))=-2{x}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{2}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{1}^{2}) & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2{x}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{2}^{2})\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\ \\sqrt{\\pi }\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{f}^{′}(a)=[\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{\\pi } & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe linear approximation of $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nf(x)≈[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{\\pi } & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}](x-[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\ \\sqrt{\\pi }\\end{aligned}]).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n-1+(-2\\sqrt{\\pi })+0=-1-2\\sqrt{\\pi }.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281624 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280844 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281624 1069 "q-163160" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281624 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281624 1071 "Consider the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}^{-1} \\\\ {x}_{1}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf the linear approximation of $f$ at $a=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈[\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}](x-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]),\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281624 1072 #uuid "2ba553fb-9d99-596b-b82a-a0b487e6030f" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281624 1074 "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}^{-1})=1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}^{-1})=-{x}_{2}^{-2} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2})={x}_{2},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2})={x}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -{x}_{2}^{-2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & {x}_{1}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{f}^{′}(a)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe linear approximation of $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nf(x)≈[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}](x-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n4+1+(-4)=1.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281625 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281267 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281625 1069 "q-163165" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281625 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281625 1071 "Consider the lamina that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}}$ and has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=y.$ Given that the mass of the lamina is $\\frac{9}{4}$ units and its center of mass is located at $(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overset{―}{x}?$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281625 1072 #uuid "53646bcf-6387-54da-9ed9-6c682d87bd18" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281625 1074 "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- ${M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- ${M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}x[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}x(\\frac{x}{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}]}_{x=0}^{x=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{9}{2})}{(\\frac{9}{4})}=2.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281626 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280912 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281626 1069 "q-163166" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281626 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281626 1071 "Consider the lamina that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{e}^{-y}\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 3}$ and has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{xy}.$ Given that the mass of the lamina is $2$ units and its center of mass is located at $(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overset{―}{y}?$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281626 1072 #uuid "7952674e-a3fc-593f-9da1-748cc35cbe0d" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281626 1074 "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- ${M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- ${M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot \\frac{1}{xy}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{{e}^{-y}}^{1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]_{x={e}^{-y}}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}0-(-y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=1}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{4}{2}=2.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281627 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281199 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281627 1069 "q-163170" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281627 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281627 1071 "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}$\nwith mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=(4y+1)\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},$ where $x$ and $y$ area measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of this lamina about the origin." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281627 1072 #uuid "e47b1623-c58d-5075-9113-1251be6ca292" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281627 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(4y+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{3}4{x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}+4{y}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3}y+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}36y+9+12{y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[18{y}^{2}+9y+3{y}^{4}+{y}^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =18+9+3+1 \\\\  & =31.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia of the lamina about the origin is $31\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281628 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280871 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281628 1069 "q-163174" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281628 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281628 1071 "![](images/a6/a6910defd242e1b65b7bdf8b580cd2f56df3ac43951a9f83f353003e00a849b4.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the lines $y=0$, $x=4$, and the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}$, as shown above. If the mass density function of $D$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=3y,$ find the moment of inertia of the lamina about the origin." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281628 1072 #uuid "566286c6-ab64-5382-ab24-0d1952795e00" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281628 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/dd/dd5ee021dd73317a775c0ea653212f7ee5545f8c6854e6a9daabdc93b5c3b9c0.png)\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot 3y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}3{x}^{2}y+3{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =96+16 \\\\  & =112.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281629 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281153 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281629 1069 "q-163178" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281629 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281629 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}.$\nThe mass density at each point is proportional to its distance from the $y$-axis.\nFind the moment of inertia of the lamina about the origin." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281629 1072 #uuid "8ef76132-2758-54ad-96e6-4961fca66327" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281629 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=k|x|=kx,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}kx\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =k({\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =k{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot [y]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =k(\\frac{1}{2}-0)(2-0) \\\\  & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=M$ and hence $\\lambda (x,y)=kx=Mx.$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{[\\frac{1}{4}y+\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =M(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11M}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280841 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281630 1069 "q-163180" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281630 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281630 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.$\nThe mass density at each point is proportional to its distance from the $x$-axis.\nFind the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $y$-axis." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281630 1072 #uuid "8ba69e53-fc8f-5143-953e-05bbe8878807" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281630 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=k|y|=ky,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}ky\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =k({\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =k[x]_{0}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =k(3-0)(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3k}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{2M}{3}$ and hence,\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=ky=\\frac{2M}{3}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{3}({\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{3}(9-0)(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =3M.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280849 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281631 1069 "q-163183" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281631 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281631 1071 "![](images/dc/dc61efd6e5ef1fd46fca53f7c1e47394c6b9c95fb2455dc0c798d9936a68631b.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the $x$-axis, $y$-axis, and the curve $y=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. If the mass density function of $D$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=10y,$ find the moment of inertia of the lamina about the origin." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281631 1072 #uuid "c204945d-1cc7-5037-a46b-3df54b66b209" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281631 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/62/626cbf8e182d9eebb8b48026f07c5dd012c1096cc9568465f3648710ea0d07a4.png)\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(10y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}10{x}^{2}y+10{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}5{x}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})+\\frac{5}{2}{(4-{x}^{2})}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5}{2}(4-{x}^{2})(2{x}^{2}+4-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}16-{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{[16x-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(32-\\frac{32}{5}) \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281632 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281245 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281632 1069 "q-163185" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281632 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281632 1071 "Consider the collection of point masses ${m}_{1}=3\\,\\text{kg},$ ${m}_{2}=1.5\\,\\text{kg},$ ${m}_{3}=4\\,\\text{kg},$ and ${m}_{4}=2\\,\\text{kg}$ located at ${x}_{1}=1\\,\\text{m},$ ${x}_{2}=2\\,\\text{m},$ ${x}_{3}=4\\,\\text{m},$ and ${x}_{4}=3\\,\\text{m},$ respectively, along the $x$-axis. Find the total moment of this system about the origin." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281632 1072 #uuid "353a7cbf-3561-53ef-84b0-fb6dc97dbc04" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281632 1074 "The moment about the origin $O$ of a collection of masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n{M}_{O}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =3, & \\,{m}_{2} & =1.5, & \\,{m}_{3} & =4, & \\,{m}_{4} & =2, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =1, & \\,{x}_{2} & =2, & \\,{x}_{3} & =4, & \\,{x}_{4} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the total moment of this system about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3}+{m}_{4}{x}_{4} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1+1.5\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3+3+16+6 \\\\  & =28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the total moment of the system is $28\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281633 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280853 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281633 1069 "q-163186" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281633 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281633 1071 "![](images/04/043119a0637985fa55a7084a8d4b67df5031b24211ab421ec019700235ca2365.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ in the first quadrant enclosed by the line $y=1-x$ and the coordinate axes. The mass density at each point is proportional to the square of its distance from the $x$-axis. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281633 1072 #uuid "0ff4be2e-d981-5594-842f-9759fc5e3f33" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281633 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/78/782b4df0220d84d90d26a4ad1fd4a34da00ecf3c5d62a9416d9f0490ca765e2b.png)\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as $\\lambda (x,y)=k{y}^{2},$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}k{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}(1-x)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{3}{[-\\frac{1}{4}(1-x)^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=12M$ and hence $\\lambda (x,y)=k{y}^{2}=12M{y}^{2}.$ Therefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =12M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}{y}^{2}({y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12M{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{5}]}_{0}^{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12M{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{5}(1-x)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{12M}{5}{[-\\frac{1}{6}(1-x)^{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12M}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281634 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281116 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281634 1069 "q-163187" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281634 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281634 1071 "Consider the collection of point masses ${m}_{1}=5\\,\\text{kg},$ ${m}_{2}=1\\,\\text{kg},$ and ${m}_{3}=4\\,\\text{kg}$ located at ${x}_{1}=1\\,\\text{m},$ ${x}_{2}=1.5\\,\\text{m},$ and ${x}_{3}=3\\,\\text{m},$ respectively, along the $x$-axis. Find the total moment of this system about the origin." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281634 1072 #uuid "b22185aa-88bc-5593-bf2f-0ef92cc1e169" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281634 1074 "The moment about the origin $O$ of a collection of masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n{M}_{O}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =5, & \\,{m}_{2} & =1, & \\,{m}_{3} & =4, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =1, & \\,{x}_{2} & =1.5, & \\,{x}_{3} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the total moment of this system about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & 