a  & =0 \\\\ r(r+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ((-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{4}-{0}^{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{[-\\frac{{cos}^{5}⁡\\theta }{5}]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}({cos}^{5}⁡\\pi -{cos}^{5}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}=\\frac{8}{5\\pi }.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281669 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281095 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281669 1069 "q-163279" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281669 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281669 1071 "![](images/71/719f86db69489f62bcbe2753e88cdc6713f04dc6f53415cc2484e2eaab8ffac3.png)\n\nFind the average value of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ on the semicircle shown above." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281669 1072 #uuid "128ce1a7-9b5d-51fb-9a3d-78ee6a5214c2" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281669 1074 "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at \n$(0,1).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x=\\sqrt{2y-{y}^{2}}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{2y-{y}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2y-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-1-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}\\cdot 1=\\frac{2}{\\pi }.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281670 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280872 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281670 1069 "q-163280" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281670 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281670 1071 "Consider a thin plate $D$ that has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)={x}^{3}$ and is enclosed between the curve $y=x$ and the $x$-axis for $0\\le x\\le 2.$ Given that the mass of the plate is $\\frac{32}{5}$ units and its center of mass is located at $(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overset{―}{y}?$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281670 1072 #uuid "319f1b6f-8e13-5060-954e-d52a8b4d9a60" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281670 1074 "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- ${M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- ${M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I plane region:\n\n![](images/a0/a042919f73d668cac20ead2752c689e57880f60bc096747f41cec3aeb3ed62ca.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le x}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)={x}^{3}.$ \n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}y{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{6}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 64 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{16}{3})}{(\\frac{32}{5})}=\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281671 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280884 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281671 1069 "q-163281" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281671 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281671 1071 "Consider a thin plate $D$ that has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ and is enclosed between the curve $y={x}^{3}$ and the $x$-axis for $1\\le x\\le 2.$ Given that the mass of the plate is $2$ units and its center of mass is located at $(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overset{―}{x}?$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281671 1072 #uuid "c9939c00-b562-5295-851c-72bc554d376f" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281671 1074 "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- ${M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- ${M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I plane region:\n\n![](images/b6/b6e8af58b8e7ba5f909d226b895bb39b3fc94e53686e4718eb32c9bb4ee61c08.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{3}}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.$ \n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot [y]_{y=0}^{y={x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281672 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281052 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281672 1069 "q-163282" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281672 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281672 1071 "Consider a thin plate $D$ that has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=x+y$ and is enclosed between the curve $x=2y$ and the $y$-axis for $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Given that the mass of the plate is $\\frac{4}{3}$ units and its center of mass is located at $(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overset{―}{x}?$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281672 1072 #uuid "a6a2919a-fe73-5a7d-8a74-b011c8911292" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281672 1074 "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- ${M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- ${M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II plane region:\n\n![](images/30/3097e540292efd601743ba8de24acffc3b51469e77561b2bccbd799d1125e1ed.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=x+y.$ \n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot (x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}{x}^{2}+xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{3}+2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{14{y}^{3}}{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{7{y}^{4}}{6}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{7}{6})}{(\\frac{4}{3})}=\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281673 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280958 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281673 1069 "q-163284" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281673 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281673 1071 "Consider the thin plate $D$ enclosed between the curve $x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}$ and the $x$-axis for $-2\\le y\\le 0.$ Given that the area of the plate is $\\pi ,$ find the $x$-coordinate of its centroid." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281673 1072 #uuid "cb3eeda9-913d-5b5e-9942-179e17b6de33" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281673 1074 "The centroid of a lamina $D$ with area ${A}_{D}$ is given by $({x}_{c},{y}_{c}),$ where\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\,\\text{d}A,\\,{y}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II plane region:\n\n![](images/14/14c292e8a224b59c143d0e8a57d43b554ce298cd4f44fc2169f516d683e8c35a.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}},\\,-2\\le y\\le 0}\n$$\n\nand ${A}_{D}=\\pi .$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinate of the centroid is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{c} & =\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{4-{y}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{\\int }_{-2}^{0}4-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{[4y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[0-(-8+\\frac{8}{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281674 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281195 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281674 1069 "q-163289" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281674 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281674 1071 "Consider the lamina that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0\\le y\\le 4}.$ Given that the area of the lamina is $\\frac{16}{3}$ square units, find the $x$-coordinate of its centroid." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281674 1072 #uuid "4e7bc910-4add-5a81-9d2e-b1278da454ee" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281674 1074 "The centroid of a lamina $D$ with area ${A}_{D}$ is given by $({x}_{c},{y}_{c}),$ where\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\,\\text{d}A,\\,{y}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinate of the centroid is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{c} & =\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281675 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281089 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281675 1069 "q-163493" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281675 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281675 1071 "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}$\nwith mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=x+y-1$ Find the moment of inertia of this plate about the line $x=2.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281675 1072 #uuid "5e32d479-ba99-5277-86cf-01366333f094" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281675 1074 "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nThe distance between a point on $D$ and the line $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|x-2|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+y-1)|x-2{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-2+y+1)(x-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-2)^{3}+(y+1)(x-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{[y(x-2)^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}(y+1)^{2}(x-2)^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(x-2)^{3}+\\frac{3}{2}(x-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{(x-2)^{4}}{4}+\\frac{(x-2)^{3}}{2}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2})-(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281676 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281222 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281676 1069 "q-163496" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281676 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281676 1071 "![](images/3f/3f9732e0b81ba2e171a0464996e7b0d27aa47a0b32da59fa40cbba0cdbf8402a.png)\n\nConsider a plate of mass $M$ that occupies the region $D$ bounded by the polar circles $r=1$ and $r=2,$ as shown above. The mass density at any point is proportional to its distance from the origin. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281676 1072 #uuid "77c3fd9b-a588-5227-b546-d84306dcef4e" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281676 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D,$ in polar coordinates, can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=kr,$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}kr\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}k{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =k({\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\frac{7}{3}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{14\\pi k}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=kr=\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }r.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }r\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }({\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }({\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}\\,{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{31}{5}\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{93M}{70}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281677 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280861 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281677 1069 "q-163501" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281677 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281677 1071 "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,1\\le y\\le 2}$\nwith mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=y-x.$ Find the moment of inertia of this plate about the line $x=-2.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281677 1072 #uuid "c5ef6472-c270-5d02-97a0-991db154ba3e" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281677 1074 "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nThe distance between a point on $D$ and the line $x=-2$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|x-(-2)|=|x+2|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $x=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(y-x)|x+2{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(y-x)(x+2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}y(x+2)^{2}-x(x+2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}(x+2)^{2}-xy(x+2)^{2}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{1}(3-2x)(x+2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{1}(-2{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+4x+12)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{-{x}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3}+2{x}^{2}+12x]}_{-2}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{5}{3}+2+12)-(-8+\\frac{40}{3}+8-24)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((14-\\frac{13}{6})-(-24+\\frac{40}{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{135}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{45}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281678 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281189 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281678 1069 "q-163503" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281678 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281678 1071 "Consider the lamina that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}.$ Given that the area of the lamina is $\\pi $ square units, find the $y$-coordinate of its centroid." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281678 1072 #uuid "832add81-6866-5228-aac7-83e3b7ca8301" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281678 1074 "The centroid of a lamina $D$ with area ${A}_{D}$ is given by $({x}_{c},{y}_{c}),$ where\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\,\\text{d}A,\\,{y}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-coordinate of the centroid is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c} & =\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}4-{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{[4x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }(8-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281679 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281030 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281679 1069 "q-163504" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281679 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281679 1071 "Consider the thin plate $D$ enclosed between the curve $x={y}^{2}$ and the $y$-axis for $-2\\le y\\le 0.$ Given that the area of the plate is $\\frac{8}{3},$ find the $y$-coordinate of its centroid." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281679 1072 #uuid "0280bbe2-b195-5319-b55c-c6602327e942" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281679 1074 "The centroid of a lamina $D$ with area ${A}_{D}$ is given by $({x}_{c},{y}_{c}),$ where\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\,\\text{d}A,\\,{y}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II plane region:\n\n![](images/a8/a8c57b12334a23542bae73ba1ac6848e69830b9663a1f83b6340f6812974f416.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2},\\,-2\\le y\\le 0}\n$$\n\nand ${A}_{D}=\\frac{8}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-coordinate of the centroid is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c} & =\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}y\\,\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}y\\,{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}y[x]_{x=0}^{x={y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}y({y}^{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}]}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot (0-4) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281680 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280964 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281680 1069 "q-163563" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281680 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281680 1071 "![](images/74/74c26773d49442671fcf6526764215523ca0231a7889671330a7ac539f3402fc.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the $y$-axis and the lines $x=2y$ and $x=3-y$, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{y}{6}.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the line $x=2.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281680 1072 #uuid "f8339842-425a-5173-a9da-9623af429ca5" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281680 1074 "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{x}{2}\\le y\\le 3-x},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/0c/0c9efcbae53bdd2620b0bf7009ce4f4f2a20a38f393cac008899621ec391d261.png)\n\nThe distance between a point on $D$ and the line $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|x-2|\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x/2}^{3-x}(\\frac{y}{6})|x-2{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x/2}^{3-x}(\\frac{y}{6})(x-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}-4x+4)[{\\int }_{x/2}^{3-x}\\frac{y}{6}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}-4x+4){[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{12}]}_{x/2}^{3-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}-4x+4)[\\frac{(3-x)^{2}}{12}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{48}]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}-4x+4)(36-24x+3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{4}-36{x}^{3}+144{x}^{2}-240x+144\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{5}}{80}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{16}+{x}^{3}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2}+3x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{5}-3+8-10+6)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281681 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281010 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281681 1069 "q-163568" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281681 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281681 1071 "![](images/55/55a781e535e1b469bc5da659eff8f82afd27a1e8bb92ccd36c2fce5a9dd49b07.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the $y$-axis and the lines $x=\\frac{4y}{3}$ and $x=4-\\frac{4y}{3}$, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is $\\lambda (x,y)=2-x.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the line $x=2.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281681 1072 #uuid "5a58e9d5-c3e2-5c20-9c17-c9c38a808e71" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281681 1074 "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{3}{4}x\\le y\\le 3-\\frac{3}{4}x},\n$$\n\n \n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/1f/1f332ebf554ac0e1b9e5099244aa50de0beef38c92efcba04e95e73abd21ecb6.png)\n\n    \n\nThe distance between a point on $D$ and the line $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|x-2|\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{3x/4}^{3-3x/4}(2-x)|x-2{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{3x/4}^{3-3x/4}{(2-x)}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2-x)^{3}[{\\int }_{3x/4}^{3-3x/4}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2-x)^{3}[y]_{3x/4}^{3-3x/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2-x)^{3}(3-\\frac{3x}{4}-\\frac{3x}{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2-x)^{4}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{[\\frac{(2-x)^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(0-\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281682 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280927 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281682 1069 "q-163576" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281682 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281682 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281682 1072 #uuid "c58b6231-e237-5de8-8e27-d0ade4f5cce7" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281682 1074 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=2\\cdot 3=6.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{3}(8-0)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9}[y]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{R}_{y}^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}\\,⇒\\,{R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{3}}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281683 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281014 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281683 1069 "q-163578" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281683 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281683 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281683 1072 #uuid "c52b9e07-a1a1-59e3-9b59-f3ce7c050afb" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281683 1074 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=1\\cdot 3=3.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{[\\frac{1}{3}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}(1+9) \\\\  & =\\frac{10M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{R}_{0}^{2}=\\frac{10}{3}\\,⇒\\,{R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{10}{3}}.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281684 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280988 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281684 1069 "q-163582" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281684 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281684 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,1\\le y\\le 3}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281684 1072 #uuid "de217583-4705-57c2-81c7-b02676db7154" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281684 1074 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=1\\cdot 2=2.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=1}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(\\frac{8}{3}+2{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{7}{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{[\\frac{7}{3}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}(7+9-\\frac{7}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}\\cdot \\frac{40}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{20M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{R}_{0}^{2}=\\frac{20}{3}\\,⇒\\,{R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{20}{3}}=2\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{3}}.\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281685 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281011 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281685 1069 "q-163583" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281685 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281685 1071 "![](images/2f/2f847a5551c0bdc5aea20673814ba6eddc10daca7661cca2b2db76c953672225.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the $y$-axis and the lines $x=y-1$ and $x=7-y$, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{y-1}{9}.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the line $x=3.$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281685 1072 #uuid "9b6b9a33-7948-5bd9-8b27-53be2933fe8e" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281685 1074 "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ about an axis of rotation $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function of the lamina, and $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the line $l$ to the point $(x,y)$ on $D.$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,x+1\\le y\\le 7-x},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/b8/b86b9a595786e544159e08fef12952c88e1bf9f796fb4ae3089e877dc9119b3f.png)\n\nThe distance between a point on $D$ and the line $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=|x-3|\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the line $x=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)[r(x,y)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{x+1}^{7-x}(\\frac{y-1}{9})|x-3{|}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{x+1}^{7-x}(\\frac{y-1}{9})(x-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(x-3)^{2}[{\\int }_{x+1}^{7-x}\\frac{y-1}{9}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(x-3)^{2}{[\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{18}]}_{x+1}^{7-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(x-3)^{2}[\\frac{(6-x)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(x-3)^{2}[\\frac{12(3-x)}{18}]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(x-3)^{3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{[\\frac{(x-3)^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(0-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281686 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281212 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281686 1069 "q-163584" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281686 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281686 1071 "A thin plate $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}$ is enclosed between the line $x=y$ and the line $x=1$ for $1\\le y\\le 2.$ Find the moment of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281686 1072 #uuid "f86bb819-7fea-5f54-bd5e-c24d69056f8c" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281686 1074 "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is a type II plane region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/6c/6cfa58d15b2a1f05aa4d4d088d44b44d6b3225084f627bc8078617d85d801957.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le y,\\,1\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot \\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{y}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y[{\\int }_{1}^{y}\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y[x]_{x=1}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y(y-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{2}-y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=1}^{y=2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{3}-2)-(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281687 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281144 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281687 1069 "q-163594" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281687 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281687 1071 "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 3.$ Its mass density at any point is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{4}{9\\pi }M{x}^{2}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $x$-axis." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281687 1072 #uuid "6b5e602d-da1f-5649-9bd5-641c23ed74ae" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281687 1074 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (r,\\theta )$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{4}{9\\pi }M{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9\\pi }M{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}{r}^{6}{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot ({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}\\cdot ({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2\\pi }\\cdot ({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta )}{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}\\theta -\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2\\pi }\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{x} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{M}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281688 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281241 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281688 1069 "q-163600" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281688 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281688 1071 "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ bounded by the curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ the line $y=1,$ and the $y$-axis. Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=12Mxy.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281688 1072 #uuid "ad1b682f-b157-528a-b0f7-855122eb923c" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281688 1074 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,\\sqrt{x}\\le y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{1}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot 12Mxy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{1}{x}^{3}y+x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{4}]}_{y=\\sqrt{x}}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12M{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12M{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{4}x+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12M{[\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =12M(\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{16}-\\frac{1}{10}) \\\\  & =12M\\cdot \\frac{7}{80} \\\\  & =\\frac{21M}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{0} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{21M}{20}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{21}{20}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\frac{21}{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281689 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281234 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281689 1069 "q-163605" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281689 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281689 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=-2$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{12}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{5}{24}$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n| | $x$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | |  | | $y$ | $-2$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ | |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $y$ | $-2$ | $2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=0,Y=-2)=P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=-2)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281689 1072 #uuid "2b24b5d8-591a-5107-953f-3194f9f2c074" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281689 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=0,Y=-2)=f(0,-2)=\\frac{1}{4}.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=-2)={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(-2)=\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,2) & =\\frac{5}{12}=\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{8}={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓ \\\\ f(3,-2) & =\\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}={f}_{X}(3)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(-2) & ✓ \\\\ f(3,2) & =\\frac{5}{24}=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{8}={f}_{X}(3)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281690 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281124 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281690 1069 "q-163609" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281690 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281690 1071 "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the lines $y=3x,$ $x=1,$ $x=2,$ and the $x$-axis. Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{45}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281690 1072 #uuid "fd3dc29c-e508-5e62-812f-85ffd3f21aae" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281690 1074 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3x}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3x}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{M}{45}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{45}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3x}{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{45}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{[{x}^{4}y+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{45}{\\int }_{1}^{2}3{x}^{5}+9{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{45}{\\int }_{1}^{2}12{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{45}[2{x}^{6}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{45}\\cdot (128-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{14M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{y} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{14M}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{14}{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281691 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281246 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281691 1069 "q-163613" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281691 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281691 1071 "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ bounded by the curve $y={x}^{3},$ the line $y=1,$ and the $y$-axis. Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{28M}{3}x{y}^{3}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $x$-axis." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281691 1072 #uuid "41aab4a9-697e-5db4-a3bf-1e7d24bbc956" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281691 1074 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{28M}{3}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{28M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{1}x{y}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{28M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{6}x{y}^{6}]}_{y={x}^{3}}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{14M}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x-{x}^{19}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{14M}{9}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{20}{x}^{20}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{14M}{9}(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{20}) \\\\  & =\\frac{14M}{9}\\cdot \\frac{9}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{7M}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{x} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{7M}{10}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{10}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281692 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281018 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281692 1069 "q-163614" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281692 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281692 1071 "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ bounded by the curves $y={x}^{3}$ and $y={x}^{2}.$ Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=20Mx.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $x$-axis." 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281692 1072 #uuid "25ae87ad-380a-5a1b-8a33-907df3e00ff1" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true] [17592186281692 1074 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le {x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{{x}^{2}}{y}^{2}\\cdot 20Mx\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =20M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{{x}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =20M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{3}x{y}^{3}]}_{y={x}^{3}}^{y={x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{20M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{7}-{x}^{10}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{20M}{3}{[\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{8}-\\frac{1}{11}{x}^{11}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{20M}{3}(\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{11}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20M}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{88} \\\\  & =\\frac{5M}{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{x} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{5M}{22}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{22}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533405 17592186045585 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0007cf9c-0395-5509-b616-4be695ec13cb" 17592186278590 "answer-002526ef-633f-58c1-b274-926fab0583fc" 17592186278591 "answer-00586df5-9953-5e7a-a58f-2329aa16a468" 17592186278592 "answer-0072a6bc-bcf0-53b9-9a48-720f5054cc41" 17592186278593 "answer-0073c988-ab13-5d35-82dc-f86a2f72a0de" 17592186278594 "answer-007e3d6a-4d9f-5068-9677-962c469778a6" 17592186278595 "answer-00b03285-dd28-53bf-99ba-9f30ee298c69" 17592186278596 "answer-00df58ac-ae19-5439-acd9-c31097afed15" 17592186278597 "answer-00ed9573-8587-5e43-a015-cff05f479c86" 17592186278598 "answer-012e5e71-30c4-576f-9027-84673f0cec11" 17592186278599 "answer-0150bb6b-2780-5777-a413-ed293109bb41" 17592186278600 "answer-01683696-1768-510b-b2ef-229cf3c6ce7b" 17592186278601 